/* ===== Go deeper: challenge content for lessons c01-c10 (G1) ===== */
(function(){ DATA.chal = DATA.chal || {};
DATA.chal['c01'] = `
<h3>Why it works</h3>
<p><b>Why inverse-square?</b> Picture the field lines from a point charge as a fixed number of "spokes" that are never created or destroyed in empty space. At radius r those spokes are spread over a sphere of area 4πr², so the density of lines (the field strength) must go as 1/r². That is why E = kQ/r² with k = 1/(4πε₀). The inverse-square law is not an arbitrary fact about charge: it is geometry in three dimensions plus conservation of field lines. (In a 2-D world the law would be 1/r.)</p>
<p><b>Units check.</b> F = k q₁q₂/r² → [k] = N·m²/C². E = F/q → N/C, and since 1 V = 1 J/C = 1 N·m/C, then N/C = V/m. The potential V = kQ/r has units (N·m²/C²)(C)/m = N·m/C = J/C = V. Good. For a uniform field, E = V/d is the same unit in disguise.</p>
<p><b>Sensitivity table</b> (each row changes one thing, all else fixed):</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Change</th><th>Force F</th><th>Field E at a point</th><th>Potential V at a point</th></tr>
<tr><td>double one charge</td><td>×2</td><td>×1 (if it is the test charge) / ×2 (if source)</td><td>×2 (source)</td></tr>
<tr><td>double both charges</td><td>×4</td><td>×2</td><td>×2</td></tr>
<tr><td>double the distance</td><td>×1/4</td><td>×1/4</td><td>×1/2</td></tr>
<tr><td>halve the distance</td><td>×4</td><td>×4</td><td>×2</td></tr>
<tr><td>both charges double AND distance halves</td><td>×16</td><td>–</td><td>–</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Charge sharing.</b> Two identical conducting spheres that touch end up at the same potential, and by symmetry that means equal charge: each gets half of the total (algebraic sum, keep signs). Total charge is conserved, but the force is generally <i>not</i> conserved: +2 and +6 (F ∝ 12) become +4 and +4 (F ∝ 16). Average charge is fixed, so the product is maximized when the charges are equal (AM-GM).</p>
<p><b>How big is the electric force really?</b> For an electron and proton, F_electric/F_gravity = k e²/(G m_e m_p) ≈ 2.3×10³⁹. That is why electricity dominates all atomic and chemical physics, and why bulk matter is nearly neutral: even a 10⁻⁹ imbalance would swamp gravity. Limiting case: a dipole (+q, −q separated by a) has field falling as 1/r³ far away, because the two 1/r² contributions almost cancel. The cancellation is also why a neutral polarizable object is attracted to a charged one: the nearer charge is closer, so it wins.</p>

<h3>Tools of thought</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Use k ≈ 9×10⁹ and round.</b> Two 1 μC charges 1 m apart: F ≈ 9×10⁹ × 10⁻¹² = 9×10⁻³ N. Anchor quantities this way to catch exponent slips (μC is 10⁻⁶, squared is 10⁻¹²).</li>
<li><b>Symmetry before arithmetic.</b> Forces and fields from symmetric charges cancel in components. Ask "which components vanish?" before computing any.</li>
<li><b>Scaling ratios.</b> "How does F change if ...?" never needs the numbers: multiply the factors (charges multiply, distance divides by its square).</li>
<li><b>Superposition with signs.</b> Between two like charges the fields oppose (a zero may exist); between opposite charges they add (no zero). An E = 0 point outside a pair of opposite charges must lie nearer the <i>smaller</i> charge.</li>
<li><b>Energy instead of force.</b> A charge q falling through ΔV gains qΔV of kinetic energy, no matter how complicated the field. For an electron, 1 V gives 1 eV = 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ J.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Challenge problems</h3>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>Two identical conducting spheres carry +2.0 μC and +6.0 μC. They are placed 0.20 m apart (centres; treat as point charges), then touched together and returned to 0.20 m. Find the force before and after.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Before: F = (8.99×10⁹)(2×10⁻⁶)(6×10⁻⁶)/0.04 = 2.70 N. After touching, charge is shared equally: +4.0 μC each. F = (8.99×10⁹)(16×10⁻¹²)/0.04 = 3.60 N (repulsive). Key insight: charge is conserved but the product q₁q₂ grew from 12 to 16 (AM-GM: for a fixed sum the product is maximized at equality). Sanity check: ratio 16/12 = 4/3, and 2.70×4/3 = 3.60.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>An electron is released from rest at the negative plate of a parallel-plate pair, 2.0 cm apart, with 500 V between them. Find E, the force and acceleration, and the speed at the positive plate (non-relativistic).</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>E = V/d = 500/0.020 = 2.5×10⁴ V/m. F = eE = (1.602×10⁻¹⁹)(2.5×10⁴) = 4.0×10⁻¹⁵ N; a = F/m = 4.0×10⁻¹⁵/9.11×10⁻³¹ = 4.4×10¹⁵ m/s². Energy shortcut: KE = eΔV = 500 eV = 8.0×10⁻¹⁷ J, so v = √(2KE/m) = 1.3×10⁷ m/s. Check: that is 4% of c, so ignoring relativity is fine; weight (mg ≈ 9×10⁻³⁰ N) is utterly negligible against 4×10⁻¹⁵ N.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>A total charge of +10.0 μC is split between two small spheres 1.00 m apart, as q and (10.0 − q) μC. The repulsive force is measured to be 0.200 N. Find the two charges. What is the largest force any split could give?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>F = k q(Q−q)/r² → q(Q−q) = F r²/k = 0.200/8.99×10⁹ = 2.225×10⁻¹¹ C². So q² − (10⁻⁵)q + 2.225×10⁻¹¹ = 0, discriminant 10⁻¹⁰ − 8.90×10⁻¹¹ = 1.10×10⁻¹¹, root 3.32×10⁻⁶, giving q = 6.66 μC and 3.34 μC (the labeling is interchangeable). The maximum force occurs at the equal split (product max): k(5×10⁻⁶)² = 0.225 N. Sanity check: 6.66 + 3.34 = 10.0 and 0.200 N is 89% of the maximum, so the charges are not far from equal. Key insight: a quadratic with a symmetric pair of roots.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>A +9.0 μC charge sits at x = 0 and a −1.0 μC charge at x = 1.00 m. Find every point on the x-axis where (a) E = 0 and (b) V = 0 (V = 0 at infinity).</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) Between the charges both fields point in +x (away from +, toward −), so they add: no zero. For x < 0 the fields oppose, but the nearer charge is the larger one, so the larger field wins: no zero. For x > 1, need 9/x² = 1/(x−1)² → 3(x−1) = x → x = 1.50 m. (b) V = 0 needs 9/|x| = 1/|x−1|. Between: 9(1−x) = x → x = 0.90 m. Beyond 1: 9(x−1) = x → x = 1.125 m. For x < 0: 9/|x| = 1/(1+|x|) has no solution. So E = 0 at one point, V = 0 at two. Key insight: E = 0 and V = 0 are different conditions (vector vs scalar) and have different locations. Check: at x = 1.5, E = 9/2.25 − 1/0.25 = 4 − 4 = 0 (in units of k×10⁻⁶).</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>Four equal charges +q sit at the corners of a square of side a. A fifth charge Q is fixed at the centre. What Q (in terms of q) leaves every corner charge with zero net force?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>By symmetry all corners are alike, and the net force on a corner points along its diagonal. Forces on a corner from the other three: two adjacent charges at distance a, each kq²/a², at right angles, resultant √2 kq²/a² outward along the diagonal; the diagonal-opposite charge at distance a√2 gives kq²/(2a²), also outward. Total outward: (kq²/a²)(√2 + 1/2). The centre charge is at a/√2, so its force is kqQ/(a²/2) = 2kqQ/a², which must pull inward, so Q is negative with 2|Q| = q(√2 + 1/2): Q = −(1 + 2√2)q/4 ≈ −0.957 q. Key insight: adding the three outward pushes along the diagonal is all that is needed. Sanity check: |Q| slightly less than q is plausible, because the centre charge sits much closer (0.71a) than the others. (This equilibrium is unstable in general: Earnshaw's theorem says electrostatic forces alone cannot give stable equilibrium.)</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>Two small balls of the same mass m = 1.0 g hang from a common point on insulating threads of length L = 0.50 m. They carry charges q₁ = 40 nC and q₂ = 10 nC. (a) Which thread makes the bigger angle with the vertical? (b) Find the separation x (small-angle approximation), then check the angle.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) Newton's third law: the electric forces on the two balls are equal in magnitude and opposite, and both have the same mass, so both threads make the <i>same</i> angle regardless of the charge ratio. (b) For each ball tanθ ≈ x/(2L) = F/(mg) with F = kq₁q₂/x². So x³ = 2kq₁q₂L/(mg) = 2(8.99×10⁹)(4×10⁻¹⁶)(0.5)/(9.8×10⁻³) = 3.67×10⁻⁴ m³ → x = 0.0716 m. Angle θ = arctan(0.0716/1.0) ≈ 4.1°, consistent with small angles (solving exactly gives x = 0.0715 m). Key insight: unequal charges do not break the symmetry. Dimension check: k q²L/(mg) is N·m²·m/N = m³.</div></details>

<h3>Trap list</h3>
<ul>
<li>Forgetting to convert μC, nC to coulombs. A factor 10⁶ or 10⁹ error in q gives 10¹² or 10¹⁸ in F.</li>
<li>Using the distance between the sphere <i>surfaces</i> instead of the centres, or using r instead of r².</li>
<li>"Force is conserved when spheres touch." Charge is conserved; the force generally changes.</li>
<li>Saying the field is zero "between" two opposite charges (they add there) or "between" two equal charges at a point that is not the midpoint.</li>
<li>Confusing E = 0 with V = 0 (vector cancellation vs scalar sum; V can be 0 where E is huge, and E = 0 where V is not 0).</li>
<li>Signs: the formula gives attraction for negative F only if you substitute signs; many tests just want direction words.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Teach-back</h3>
<p>Explain Coulomb's law to a friend in 3 sentences: say why it is inverse-square (field lines spread over a sphere), what doubling each thing does, and why charge sharing between touching identical spheres is always equal.</p>
`;
DATA.chal['c02'] = `
<h3>Why it works</h3>
<p><b>C = Q/V is a geometric fact.</b> For a given shape of conductors, the field everywhere is proportional to the charge on them, so the voltage (an integral of E) is proportional to Q. The ratio depends only on geometry and the material between the plates. For parallel plates E = σ/ε = Q/(εA) and V = Ed, so C = εA/d. The units check: ε₀ is F/m, times m²/m gives F. Bigger area collects more charge for the same push; bigger gap makes the same charge need more voltage.</p>
<p><b>Why capacitors add "backwards".</b> In parallel the plate areas add, so C adds. In series the gaps add (and Q is the same on each), so 1/C adds: V = Q/C₁ + Q/C₂ = Q/C_eq. Resistors are the mirror image because conductances add in parallel.</p>
<p><b>Energy derivation.</b> Charging from q to q + dq against voltage q/C costs dW = (q/C) dq. Integrating from 0 to Q gives U = Q²/(2C) = ½CV² = ½QV. The ½ is the average voltage during charging. Units: F·V² = (C/V)V² = C·V = J.</p>
<p><b>Why RC charging is an exponential.</b> The current is i = (V_s − v)/R and i = C dv/dt, so dv/dt is proportional to how far v is from its target. "Rate proportional to the gap" always gives an exponential with time constant τ = RC (units: Ω·F = (V/A)(C/V) = C/A = s). Every τ closes 63.2% of the remaining gap.</p>
<p><b>Energy bookkeeping you must know.</b> Charging a capacitor from a battery through any resistor: the battery supplies QV_s = CV_s², the capacitor stores ½CV_s², and the resistor dissipates the other ½CV_s², <i>independent of the value of R</i>. (Check: ∫i²R dt with i = (V_s/R)e^(−t/RC) equals ½CV_s².) If R → 0 the energy does not vanish: it goes into radiation and sparks.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Double ...</th><th>C</th><th>τ = RC</th><th>Stored U at fixed V</th><th>Stored U at fixed Q</th></tr>
<tr><td>plate area</td><td>×2</td><td>×2</td><td>×2</td><td>×1/2</td></tr>
<tr><td>plate gap</td><td>×1/2</td><td>×1/2</td><td>×1/2</td><td>×2</td></tr>
<tr><td>dielectric constant</td><td>×2</td><td>×2</td><td>×2</td><td>×1/2</td></tr>
<tr><td>voltage</td><td>×1</td><td>×1</td><td>×4</td><td>–</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Tools of thought</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>t = 0 and t = ∞ shortcuts.</b> Uncharged capacitor = wire at t = 0; charged capacitor = open circuit at ∞. Most "find the current at the instant the switch closes" problems are a resistor network with capacitors shorted.</li>
<li><b>Charge is the conserved quantity.</b> For isolated plates or nodes connected through capacitors, write conservation of charge (sum of Q on the joined plates) rather than conservation of voltage.</li>
<li><b>Fixed V vs fixed Q.</b> Ask whether the battery is still attached. Attached: V fixed, Q changes. Disconnected: Q fixed, V changes. Energy changes in opposite directions for the same act (inserting a dielectric).</li>
<li><b>Convert times to τ units.</b> t = −τ ln(1 − f) with ln 2 = 0.693, ln 3 = 1.099, ln 10 = 2.303. Energy ∝ v², so the energy half-life is half the voltage half-life.</li>
<li><b>Voltage divides inversely with C in series</b> (equal charge): the smaller C gets more voltage, so it breaks down first.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Challenge problems</h3>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>A 10 μF capacitor is charged to 12 V, disconnected, and then connected across an uncharged 20 μF capacitor (same polarity). Find the final voltage and the energy lost.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Charge is conserved: Q = 10 μF × 12 V = 120 μC shared over C = 30 μF, so V = 4.0 V. Initial energy ½(10 μF)(144) = 720 μJ. Final ½(30 μF)(16) = 240 μJ. Lost: 480 μJ, two-thirds of the original, even though there was "no resistor" (the wires, sparks and radiation take it). Key insight: connecting capacitors by wire is never energy-conserving unless the voltages already match. Check: V = C₁V₁/(C₁+C₂) = 12/3 = 4.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>R = 22 kΩ charges C = 100 μF from a 5.0 V supply. (a) Find τ. (b) How long until the capacitor reaches 90% of 5.0 V? (c) After being fully charged to 12 V, how long to discharge through the same resistor to 3.0 V?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) τ = 22×10³ × 100×10⁻⁶ = 2.2 s. (b) 0.90 = 1 − e^(−t/τ) → t = τ ln 10 = 2.2 × 2.303 = 5.07 s. (c) 3 = 12 e^(−t/τ) → t = τ ln 4 = 2.2 × 1.386 = 3.05 s. Check: at 5τ = 11 s it would be essentially done; 90% takes 2.3τ, which matches the rule-of-thumb table (86.5% at 2τ, 95% at 3τ).</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>Two capacitors, 10 μF and 20 μF, each rated for 50 V, are connected in series. What is the largest safe voltage across the pair?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Trap: 50 + 50 = 100 V is wrong. In series both carry the same Q and the smaller C gets the larger voltage. The 10 μF reaches 50 V at Q = 500 μC; at that charge the 20 μF is at 500/20 = 25 V. Total = 75 V. (If you instead let the 20 μF reach 50 V, Q = 1000 μC and the 10 μF would see 100 V, which breaks it.) Key insight: find the limiting capacitor by charge rating, Q_max = C·V_rating: 500 μC vs 1000 μC, the smaller wins. Check: 500 μC / C_eq(6.67 μF) = 75 V.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>A parallel-plate capacitor, C = 2.0 μF in air, is charged to 10 V. A dielectric slab with ε_r = 4 is slid in to fill the gap. Find the stored energy before and after in two cases: (a) the battery stays connected, (b) the battery was disconnected first. In each case, is the dielectric pulled in or pushed out?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Before: ½(2 μF)(100) = 100 μJ. (a) Battery connected: V stays 10 V, C = 8 μF, U = 400 μJ. Q goes from 20 μC to 80 μC, so the battery moves ΔQ = 60 μC through 10 V and supplies 600 μJ. Stored energy rose by 300 μJ, so 300 μJ went into work done by the field on the slab: it is pulled in. (b) Disconnected: Q = 20 μC is fixed, C = 8 μF, U = Q²/2C = 25 μJ. Energy fell by 75 μJ, which again means the slab is pulled in (and V drops to 2.5 V). Key insight: the answer to "pulled in or not" is the same for both, even though stored energy goes up in (a) and down in (b); you must track the battery's work. Check: (a) 600 = 300 + 300.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>A C = 100 μF capacitor is charged from 0 to 10 V from ideal sources through resistors. (a) Charged in one step from a 10 V source, how much energy is dissipated in the resistor? (b) Charged in 10 equal steps, with the source voltage raised 1 V at a time (each time waiting for equilibrium), how much is dissipated in total?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) Dissipated = ½CV² = 0.5 × 100×10⁻⁶ × 100 = 5.0 mJ (equal to what is stored, regardless of R). (b) Each step sets a source voltage ΔV above the capacitor, and each step dissipates ½C(ΔV)² = 0.5 × 10⁻⁴ × 1 = 50 μJ. Ten steps: 0.50 mJ. In general, with N equal steps the loss is ½CV²/N, which goes to 0 as N grows: slow ("adiabatic") charging is efficient. Key insight: the loss depends on the voltage jump, not on R. The same idea is used in low-power digital logic.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>Capacitor A (4 μF) is charged to 9 V and capacitor B (6 μF) to 6 V. They are connected in parallel (a) with the same polarity, (b) with opposite polarity (A's + to B's −). Find the final voltage and the energy lost in each case.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Charges: Q_A = 36 μC, Q_B = 36 μC. Initial energy: ½(4)(81) + ½(6)(36) = 162 + 108 = 270 μJ. (a) Same polarity: charge adds: 72 μC over 10 μF → 7.2 V; U = ½(10)(51.84) = 259.2 μJ; lost 10.8 μJ. (b) Opposite: charge cancels exactly: 36 − 36 = 0, so V = 0 and all 270 μJ is lost. Key insight: the equal and opposite charges are the point; the voltages (9 V and 6 V) differ, which tempts you to subtract them, but the quantity that adds is charge, not voltage. Check: in (a) the loss formula ½(C₁C₂/(C₁+C₂))(V₁−V₂)²: with V₁ − V₂ = 3 V, 0.5 × 2.4 μF × 9 V² = 10.8 μJ ✓.</div></details>

<h3>Trap list</h3>
<ul>
<li>Adding series capacitors like series resistors (or vice versa). Series C combines like parallel R.</li>
<li>Using voltage conservation when capacitors are joined; it is the <i>charge</i> that is conserved (with signs).</li>
<li>Forgetting C in farads: 47 μF = 47×10⁻⁶, not 47×10⁻³. τ in ms vs s is a common slip.</li>
<li>"5τ means 100% charged." It is 99.3%, not 100%, and the capacitor never truly finishes.</li>
<li>Saying the 63% rule applies to the current: current <i>falls</i> to 36.8% in one τ during charging.</li>
<li>Saying energy is conserved when two capacitors at different voltages are joined (it is not; half or more is lost).</li>
<li>Series ratings: two 50 V capacitors do not give 100 V unless their C are equal.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Teach-back</h3>
<p>Explain to a friend in 3 sentences why a capacitor acts like a wire at t = 0 and an open circuit at t = ∞, and why the charging energy loss in the resistor does not depend on the resistor.</p>
`;
DATA.chal['c03'] = `
<h3>Why it works</h3>
<p><b>Ohm's law as a microscopic statement.</b> Charge carriers in a metal constantly collide with the lattice, so a steady field E produces a steady average drift speed v_d proportional to E (like terminal velocity in a fluid). Then the current density is J = n q v_d = σE. A wire of length L and area A carries I = JA and has V = EL, so V/I = L/(σA) = ρL/A. Ohm's law, V = IR with R = ρL/A, is just "velocity proportional to force" under friction. That is also why it fails when the friction law changes (a hot filament, a diode).</p>
<p><b>Power from energy per charge.</b> A charge q dropping through V loses qV of energy. Current is charge per second, so the energy per second is P = VI. Units: (J/C)(C/s) = J/s = W. Substituting V = IR or I = V/R gives P = I²R = V²/R. Which form to use depends on what is held fixed: <i>V fixed</i> (mains, battery): P = V²/R, power falls with R. <i>I fixed</i> (series chain): P = I²R, power rises with R. That single distinction settles "which bulb is brighter" questions.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Change</th><th>I</th><th>P if V is fixed</th><th>P if I is fixed</th></tr>
<tr><td>R doubles</td><td>×1/2 (V fixed)</td><td>×1/2</td><td>×2</td></tr>
<tr><td>V doubles (R fixed)</td><td>×2</td><td>×4</td><td>–</td></tr>
<tr><td>wire length ×2, same area</td><td>R ×2</td><td>×1/2</td><td>×2</td></tr>
<tr><td>wire diameter ×2, same length</td><td>R ×1/4</td><td>×4</td><td>×1/4</td></tr>
<tr><td>stretch wire to length ×n (volume fixed)</td><td>R ×n²</td><td>×1/n²</td><td>×n²</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Units and limits.</b> Ω = V/A, S = A/V. ρ·L/A has units (Ω·m)(m)/m² = Ω. As R → 0, I = V/R → ∞ (a short circuit; the real limit is the source's internal resistance). As R → ∞, I → 0 and all the voltage appears across the open gap: that is why a voltmeter across an open switch reads the full battery voltage.</p>
<p><b>Drift is slow, signals are fast.</b> I = nqAv_d, so for 10 A in a 2 mm² copper wire v_d is under a millimetre per second. The bulb lights "instantly" because the field, which sets all the carriers moving at once, travels at a large fraction of c. Electrons carry the charge; the field carries the news.</p>

<h3>Tools of thought</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Choose the power formula from the constraint.</b> Constant V (parallel loads): P = V²/R. Constant I (series loads): P = I²R. A 100 W and 60 W bulb in series: the 60 W one (higher R) is brighter.</li>
<li><b>Scaling instead of numbers.</b> "Wire B is twice the length and half the diameter of wire A": R ×2 × 4 = ×8. Write the ratio, cancel everything else.</li>
<li><b>Conserved volume.</b> Stretching or squeezing a wire keeps volume, so A·L is fixed and R ∝ L². Bending/cutting conserves length, changing only the arrangement.</li>
<li><b>Units as an error detector.</b> If your answer to a current question comes out in Ω·s, you divided the wrong way. Check mA vs A and kΩ vs Ω first; most wrong answers are off by a power of 1000.</li>
<li><b>Energy-cost estimation.</b> 1 kW for 1 h = 1 kWh = 3.6 MJ. A 1500 W heater for 8 h at $0.15/kWh is 12 kWh × 0.15 = $1.80.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Challenge problems</h3>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>A copper wire has resistance 2.0 Ω. It is drawn out (volume unchanged) to three times its original length. What is its new resistance?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Length ×3 gives R ×3, and since volume is constant the area falls to A/3, giving another ×3. Total ×9: 2.0 × 9 = 18 Ω. Key insight: R ∝ L²/(volume) at fixed volume. Check: if the wire were just three times longer with the same area (three segments in series) it would be 6 Ω; the thinning adds a further factor 3.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>A heater is labelled 1500 W at 120 V. Find its current and resistance. During a brownout the supply sags to 100 V. What power does it draw (assume constant resistance), and what fraction of rated power is that?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>I = P/V = 1500/120 = 12.5 A. R = V²/P = 14 400/1500 = 9.6 Ω. At 100 V: P = V²/R = 10 000/9.6 = 1042 W. The fraction is (100/120)² = 0.694. Key insight: a 17% voltage drop costs 31% of the power, because power goes as V². Check: I = 100/9.6 = 10.4 A and P = 100 × 10.4 = 1042 W ✓. (Real heating elements change R slightly with temperature, which is ignored here.)</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>A 60 W and a 100 W bulb, both rated for 120 V, are connected in series across 120 V. (a) Which is brighter? (b) What power does each dissipate (assume the rated-voltage resistances) and what is the total?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Ratings give R = V²/P: R₆₀ = 240 Ω, R₁₀₀ = 144 Ω. In series the same current flows, so P = I²R favours the larger resistance: the <i>60 W</i> bulb is brighter. I = 120/(240 + 144) = 0.3125 A. P₆₀ = 0.3125² × 240 = 23.4 W and P₁₀₀ = 0.3125² × 144 = 14.1 W. Total = 37.5 W, far below the 160 W you would get by adding the ratings. Key insight: a "bigger wattage" bulb is a <i>smaller</i> resistance; the rule "more watts means brighter" holds only at equal voltage. Check: total = V²/R_total = 14 400/384 = 37.5 W ✓.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>A 2.0 mm² copper wire carries 10 A. Copper has n = 8.5×10²⁸ free electrons per m³. Find the drift speed and how long an electron needs to drift 1 m.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>v_d = I/(nqA) = 10/[(8.5×10²⁸)(1.602×10⁻¹⁹)(2.0×10⁻⁶)] = 3.7×10⁻⁴ m/s = 0.37 mm/s. One metre takes 1/3.7×10⁻⁴ ≈ 2700 s ≈ 45 min. Key insight: the lamp does not wait for electrons to arrive from the switch; the electric field sets the whole wire in motion almost at once. Unit check: A/(m⁻³·C·m²) = (C/s)/(C/m) = m/s ✓. Sanity check: the random thermal speeds are ~10⁵–10⁶ m/s, so the drift is a tiny bias on top.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>A fuse wire of diameter 0.20 mm blows at 3.0 A. Assume that in steady state the wire reaches the melting temperature when heat generated equals heat lost from its surface, and that heat loss per unit surface area at the melting point is the same for any diameter. What current blows a fuse wire of the same metal with diameter 0.40 mm?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Heat generated per unit length is I²R' = I²ρ/(πd²/4) ∝ I²/d². Heat lost per unit length is (surface area per length) × (constant) ∝ πd ∝ d. At fusing these are equal: I²/d² ∝ d, so I ∝ d^(3/2). Doubling d multiplies I by 2^(3/2) = 2.83: 3.0 × 2.83 = 8.5 A (not 12 A, which naive area scaling would suggest). Key insight: volume (heating) grows like d², surface (cooling) like d: thick wires run relatively hotter, so current capacity grows slower than area. This is Preece's law for fuses, and the same reason a thick cable needs disproportionately more current to heat up. Length drops out.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>A 60 W, 120 V bulb has a hot filament resistance of 240 Ω. Its cold (20 °C) resistance is about one-tenth of that. (a) What surge current flows at switch-on? (b) Using the linear law R = R₀[1 + α(T − T₀)] with α = 4.5×10⁻³ per °C for tungsten, estimate the filament temperature rise. (c) Why do bulbs fail at switch-on?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) R₀ ≈ 24 Ω, so I_surge = 120/24 = 5.0 A, ten times the steady 0.50 A (the surge decays in a fraction of a second as the filament heats). (b) 240 = 24[1 + 0.0045 ΔT] → 1 + 0.0045ΔT = 10 → ΔT = 9/0.0045 = 2000 °C, so T ≈ 2000 °C (the true temperature is somewhat higher because α falls at high T; the linear law is only approximate, but the estimate lands in the right range for a filament). (c) At switch-on the filament briefly sees 10× the steady power (P = V²/R = 600 W), and any thin weak spot gets hottest first. Key insight: on a fixed-voltage source a low <i>cold</i> resistance means a large surge; the same reasoning is why inrush-current limiters (NTC thermistors) exist.</div></details>

<h3>Trap list</h3>
<ul>
<li>"More watts on the label = brighter in series." No: for equal current, higher R is brighter.</li>
<li>Using P = V²/R with the <i>source</i> voltage when the load is only part of the circuit; V must be the voltage <i>across that element</i>.</li>
<li>Doubling voltage doubles power. It quadruples it (at fixed R).</li>
<li>Forgetting that stretching a wire also thins it (R ∝ L²), or that doubling the <i>diameter</i> (not radius-area) divides R by 4.</li>
<li>Saying electrons flow through a wire at the speed of light; the drift speed is millimetres per second.</li>
<li>Cost questions: W vs kW, and time in hours (kWh), not seconds. 1 kWh = 3.6×10⁶ J.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Teach-back</h3>
<p>Explain Ohm's law to a friend in 3 sentences: say what V, I and R each mean physically (push, flow, friction), which power formula you use in series versus parallel, and why electrons are slow but the bulb lights instantly.</p>
`;
DATA.chal['c04'] = `
<h3>Why it works</h3>
<p><b>The code is scientific notation with colours.</b> Two (or three) significant digits times a power of ten: digit digit × 10^multiplier. That is why the multiplier band is an <i>exponent</i>: orange (3) means ×10³, not ×3. Gold and silver give negative exponents (×10⁻¹, ×10⁻²), which is why they appear as multipliers only for values below 10 Ω. The tolerance band is placed at the far end, separated by a gap, so you always know which end to start from. Units check: the result of digits × multiplier is directly in ohms.</p>
<p><b>Why the preferred values are 10, 12, 15, 18, 22, 27, 33, 39, 47, 56, 68, 82.</b> Manufacturers do not stock all values; they stock a geometric ladder: each step is the same <i>ratio</i>, so that with the tolerance the ranges tile the decade with no gaps. For the E12 series the ratio is 10^(1/12) = 1.2115 (about 21% per step). The raw values 10, 12.1, 14.7, 17.8, 21.5, 26.1, 31.6, 38.3, 46.4, 56.2, 68.1, 82.5 are rounded to two digits (with a few historical adjustments such as 27, 33, 39, 47). The same idea gives E6 (10^(1/6), 20%), E24 (10^(1/24) = 1.1007, 5%) and E96 (1.0243, 1%). Why a ratio and not a constant step? A 1 Ω step is huge at 2 Ω but invisible at 2 kΩ: what matters is the <i>percentage</i> spacing because tolerance is a percentage.</p>
<p><b>Tolerance coverage condition.</b> The bands R(1 ± t) and 1.2115R(1 ± t) just touch when (1 + t) = 1.2115(1 − t), i.e. t = (ρ − 1)/(ρ + 1) with ρ the step ratio. For E12: t = 9.6% (hence ±10%). For E24: 4.8% (±5%). For E6: 19% (±20%).</p>
<p><b>Combining tolerances.</b> For a sum, absolute errors add, so the percentage error of a series string is the <i>weighted average</i> of the individual percentages (weighted by value). For resistors in parallel, the conductances add and the same weighted-average rule applies to conductance. For a ratio such as a divider, relative errors in numerator and denominator subtract in the worst case, but are scaled by how far the ratio is from 0 or 1.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Combination</th><th>Worst-case tolerance</th></tr>
<tr><td>two equal 5% resistors in series</td><td>5%</td></tr>
<tr><td>two equal 5% resistors in parallel</td><td>5%</td></tr>
<tr><td>R₁ (t₁) + R₂ (t₂) in series</td><td>(R₁t₁ + R₂t₂)/(R₁ + R₂)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1:1 divider, each 5%</td><td>5% on V_out (not 10%)</td></tr>
<tr><td>product or quotient of two quantities</td><td>t₁ + t₂ (first order)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Tools of thought</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Sanity-check the magnitude.</b> Yellow-violet-red is 47×10² = 4.7 kΩ: digits 4, 7 then "two zeros". If your answer is 4.7 MΩ, re-read the multiplier band.</li>
<li><b>Tolerance as an interval.</b> Always convert "R ± t%" to [R(1−t), R(1+t)]. Then "is this measurement acceptable?" is a simple inequality. 4.65 kΩ is inside 4.7 kΩ ±5% (4.465–4.935).</li>
<li><b>Orientation test.</b> A gold or silver band can only be the tolerance band, so it must be on the right. If the first band (left) is gold, you are reading it backwards.</li>
<li><b>Worst case vs typical case.</b> Specs give worst case. Random errors from many parts partially cancel, so the real spread of a 10-resistor sum is smaller than the sum of the tolerances.</li>
<li><b>SMD codes are the same exponent code in numbers.</b> "472" = 47 × 10² = 4.7 kΩ; "R47" = 0.47 Ω.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Challenge problems</h3>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>A resistor is banded Brown–Black–Orange–Gold. (a) What are its nominal value and tolerance window? (b) A meter reads 10.62 kΩ with the resistor out of circuit. Is it in tolerance?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) Brown 1, Black 0, Orange ×10³: 10 × 1000 = 10 kΩ, gold = ±5%. Window: 10 000 × 0.95 = 9500 Ω to 10 500 Ω. (b) 10.62 kΩ exceeds 10.50 kΩ, so it is out of tolerance (+6.2%). Key insight: the window is a fixed interval computed from the nominal, not from the reading. A reading 6% high would be acceptable for a ±10% part, so the tolerance band matters.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>A five-band resistor reads Yellow–Violet–Black–Red–Brown. Decode it, give the tolerance window, and find the current when 9.0 V is applied.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Yellow 4, Violet 7, Black 0, Red ×10²: 470 × 100 = 47 000 Ω = 47 kΩ, brown = ±1%. Window: 46.53 kΩ to 47.47 kΩ. I = 9.0/47 000 = 0.191 mA (0.190 to 0.193 mA across the window). Key insight: in a five-band code the third band is a digit, so the multiplier is the fourth; counting bands first prevents mis-decoding. Check: a four-band misread (47 × 10⁰ = 47 Ω) would give 190 mA, a thousand times too large.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>A 1.00 kΩ ±5% resistor is in series with a 10.0 kΩ ±1% resistor. Find the nominal series resistance and the worst-case range, and express the range as a percentage tolerance.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Nominal: 11.0 kΩ. Maximum: 1.05 + 10.1 = 11.15 kΩ. Minimum: 0.95 + 9.9 = 10.85 kΩ. Tolerance = 0.15/11 = 1.36% (±). This is the weighted average (1000 × 5% + 10 000 × 1%)/11 000 = 1.36%. Key insight: the big resistor dominates the error budget, so the loosest tolerance in a series string matters only in proportion to its share of the total. Check: the tolerance is between 1% and 5%, and much closer to 1%.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>Two 10 kΩ ±5% resistors form a divider from a 5.00 V supply (nothing loads the output). What is the worst-case range of V_out? Explain why it is not ±10%.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Nominal V_out = 2.500 V. Maximum: bottom resistor high, top low: 5 × 10.5/(10.5 + 9.5) = 2.625 V. Minimum: bottom low, top high: 5 × 9.5/20 = 2.375 V. So ±0.125 V, which is ±5%. The divider ratio x = R₂/(R₁+R₂) has relative sensitivity (1 − x)(δR₂/R₂ − δR₁/R₁); with x = 0.5 and both errors ±5% the worst case is 0.5 × 10% = 5%. Key insight: errors in top and bottom resistors partly cancel because the ratio only cares about their <i>difference</i>; if both were 5% high together, V_out would not move at all. Check: a divider made from a matched pair on one tape (same drift) is far better than the worst case.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>The E-series resistor values are spaced geometrically with ratio ρ = 10^(1/N). For full coverage of a decade, the ±t% bands of adjacent values must overlap. (a) Find the smallest t that gives coverage for E6, E12 and E24. (b) E96 parts are sold at ±1%; is the decade fully covered?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Adjacent values R and ρR have bands that just touch when R(1 + t) = ρR(1 − t), so t = (ρ − 1)/(ρ + 1). E6: ρ = 1.4678 → t = 18.96% (hence ±20%). E12: ρ = 1.2115 → t = 9.56% (hence ±10%). E24: ρ = 1.1007 → t = 4.79% (hence ±5%). (b) E96: ρ = 1.0243 → t = 1.20%, which is larger than 1%, so there are small gaps between the ±1% bands; the gap between adjacent bands is about 0.4% of the value (upper edge 1.0100 versus next lower edge 1.0140). Key insight: each series is designed so that its standard tolerance roughly matches the spacing, because a finer ladder than the tolerance buys nothing. Check: E6 to E24 land on the familiar ±20/10/5% pairing.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>You want 2.00 kΩ to within ±1% and have a bin of 1 kΩ ±5% resistors. Assume each resistor's actual value is uniformly distributed over its ±5% window, independently. If you take two at random and connect them in series, what is the probability that the pair is within ±1% of 2.00 kΩ?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Let the percentage deviations be x and y, each uniform on [−5, 5]. The series value deviates by (x + y)/2 percent (equal resistors, so equal weights). We need |x + y|/2 ≤ 1, i.e. |x + y| ≤ 2. On the 10 × 10 square of possibilities, the region |x + y| > 2 consists of two right triangles with legs 10 − 2 = 8: area 2 × 32 = 64. So the favourable area is 100 − 64 = 36 and the probability is 36/100 = 0.36. Key insight: averaging two parts narrows the error distribution (triangular, peak at 0): the pair is within 1% 36% of the time, versus 20% for a single part being within 1% of nominal (a 2%-wide window out of 10%). A simulation of two million pairs gives 0.359. Check: P(|x| ≤ 1) = 2/10 = 0.20 for one resistor, so 0.36 is plausible.</div></details>

<h3>Trap list</h3>
<ul>
<li>Reading the code backwards. Gold/silver at the left end is the telltale; the tolerance band is also often spaced a bit apart.</li>
<li>Taking the multiplier band as the multiplier itself (orange = ×3) instead of the exponent (×10³).</li>
<li>Counting 5-band resistors as 4-band: the third band is a <i>digit</i>, not the multiplier.</li>
<li>Applying the tolerance percentage to the reading instead of the nominal value.</li>
<li>Assuming tolerances add in a divider, or in a parallel combination; they partially cancel in ratios and weight by value in sums.</li>
<li>Measuring a resistor while it is still in circuit, or pinching both leads with your fingers: parallel paths make the reading too low.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Teach-back</h3>
<p>Explain the resistor colour code to a friend in 3 sentences: digits then a power-of-ten multiplier, which end the tolerance sits on, and why standard values are spaced by a fixed ratio rather than a fixed step.</p>
`;
DATA.chal['c05'] = `
<h3>Why it works</h3>
<p><b>Series and parallel are two conservation laws.</b> In series there is only one path, so charge conservation forces the same current through every element: V = I(R₁ + R₂ + ...). In parallel every branch connects the same two nodes, and the voltage between two nodes does not depend on the path (energy per charge is path-independent), so every branch has the same V. Currents add, so <i>conductances</i> add: 1/R_eq = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + ... . Units check: 1/Ω = S = A/V, so G·V is a current. Product-over-sum, R₁R₂/(R₁+R₂), is the same statement for two branches.</p>
<p><b>The dividers come from the same two rules.</b> Voltage divider (series, same I): V₂ = IR₂ = V_in R₂/(R₁+R₂). Current divider (parallel, same V): I₁ = V/R₁ = I_tot R₂/(R₁+R₂). Notice the swap in the current divider: the <i>other</i> resistor appears in the numerator, so the smaller resistor carries the larger current.</p>
<p><b>Limiting cases as a built-in checker.</b> Parallel R_eq is always below the smallest branch; series R_eq is always above the largest. As R₂ → ∞ in a divider, V_out → V_in (open output); as R₂ → 0, V_out → 0. In the parallel of two resistors where one is much larger, R_eq ≈ the smaller one, with correction 1/(1 + R_small/R_large): 1 kΩ ∥ 100 kΩ = 990 Ω.</p>
<p><b>Monotonicity (a bounding weapon).</b> Raising any one resistor in a network can never lower the resistance between any two terminals; lowering one can never raise it. Hence: cutting a wire cannot lower R_eq, and shorting two nodes together cannot raise it. So R_eq(with the bridge resistor present) lies between R_eq(bridge removed) and R_eq(bridge shorted). This squeezes hard networks to two easy ones, and gives you sure bounds when exact work is too long.</p>
<p><b>Loading.</b> A divider with output unloaded gives V_in R₂/(R₁+R₂). A load R_L is in parallel with R₂, so R₂ is replaced by R₂∥R_L. The load is noticed when R_L is comparable to R₂, invisible when R_L ≥ 100 R₂ (error about 1%).</p>
<table>
<tr><th>n equal resistors R</th><th>Series</th><th>Parallel</th></tr>
<tr><td>R_eq</td><td>nR</td><td>R/n</td></tr>
<tr><td>what is shared</td><td>same I</td><td>same V</td></tr>
<tr><td>total power on fixed V</td><td>V²/(nR)</td><td>nV²/R</td></tr>
<tr><td>total power at fixed I</td><td>nI²R</td><td>I²R/n</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Symmetry.</b> Two points that the circuit treats identically (mirror images about the source axis) sit at the same potential. No current flows between equipotential points, so any wire or resistor joining them can be removed or shorted without changing anything else. This is the master key to cubes, tetrahedra, rings and bridges.</p>

<h3>Tools of thought</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Redraw by node labels.</b> Name every distinct node (a wire is one node) and rewrite the circuit as a list of "R between X and Y". Parallel resistors are simply those with the same label pair.</li>
<li><b>Equipotential shortcut.</b> Identify equal-potential nodes by symmetry or by a balanced bridge (R₁/R₂ = R₃/R₄) and delete the joining element. 10 Ω across a balanced bridge can be any value: it carries nothing.</li>
<li><b>Fold by symmetry.</b> A mirror line lets you split a node or resistor in two: a resistor on the axis is equivalent to two resistors of 2R in parallel (each half of the current).</li>
<li><b>Self-similarity.</b> An infinite ladder looks the same after removing the first stage: R_eq = R_series + R_shunt∥R_eq. Solve a quadratic and discard the negative root.</li>
<li><b>Bounds and Foster's check.</b> Use removal/shorting to bracket; use Foster's theorem (below) to test a result.</li>
<li><b>Ring formula.</b> In a ring of N equal resistors, the resistance between two nodes k steps apart is R·k(N − k)/N: the two arcs in parallel (product over sum).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Challenge problems</h3>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>A 12 V source feeds a divider of two 10 kΩ resistors in series (top and bottom). A 10 kΩ load is connected across the bottom resistor. Find V_out with and without the load, and the power dissipated in the top resistor with the load.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Unloaded: V_out = 12 × 10/20 = 6.0 V. Loaded: the bottom resistor becomes 10 ∥ 10 = 5 kΩ, so V_out = 12 × 5/(10 + 5) = 4.0 V. Top resistor carries I = 12/15 kΩ = 0.80 mA (the total current), so P = I²R = (0.8×10⁻³)² × 10⁴ = 6.4 mW. Key insight: a load comparable to the divider resistor drags V_out down by a third; the divider formula is only valid if the output draws negligible current. Check: the top drop is 12 − 4 = 8 V and 8 V × 0.8 mA = 6.4 mW ✓.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>Three identical bulbs: bulb A is in series with the parallel pair B and C, all across one battery. (a) Rank the brightness. (b) Bulb C is unscrewed. What happens to A and B?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) A carries the full current I; B and C each carry I/2. Power ∝ I²R, so A has (I)², B and C have (I/2)² = 1/4 of that: A is 4 times as bright as B or C, and B = C. (b) Before: R_total = 1.5R, I = V/(1.5R), P_A = 4V²/(9R) = 0.444 V²/R, P_B = 0.111 V²/R. After C is removed: R_total = 2R, I = V/(2R), so P_A = P_B = 0.25 V²/R. A <i>dims</i> (0.444 → 0.25) and B <i>brightens</i> (0.111 → 0.25), and the two become equal. Key insight: removing a parallel branch raises the total resistance, which lowers the source current; the remaining branch now carries all of it. Check: V_A + V_B: after removal each bulb has V/2, so P = (V/2)²/R = 0.25V²/R ✓.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>A bridge has four arms: 6 Ω (top left) and 12 Ω (bottom left) on the left side, 4 Ω (top right) and 8 Ω (bottom right) on the right side, all joined to the source terminals at the top and bottom. A 100 Ω resistor joins the midpoints of the left and right sides. Find the resistance seen by the source.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Check balance: top/bottom ratio on the left is 6/12 = 1/2 and on the right 4/8 = 1/2. The midpoints sit at the same potential (both divide the source voltage in ratio 1:2), so no current flows through the 100 Ω. It can be removed. What remains is two series strings in parallel: (6 + 12) ∥ (4 + 8) = 18 ∥ 12 = 18 × 12/30 = 7.2 Ω. Key insight: the value 100 Ω is a decoy; any value (even a short circuit) gives the same answer. Check: a nodal solution with the 100 Ω included also gives 7.2 Ω.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>An infinite ladder has a 2.0 Ω resistor in series along the top rail of every stage and a 6.0 Ω resistor from the top rail to the bottom rail (a shunt) in every stage. (a) Find the input resistance. (b) With 10 V applied at the input, what is the voltage across the third shunt resistor?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) Removing the first stage leaves the same infinite ladder with input resistance R_x. So R_x = 2 + 6R_x/(6 + R_x). Multiply out: R_x(6 + R_x) = 2(6 + R_x) + 6R_x, so R_x² − 2R_x − 12 = 0, R_x = 1 + √13 = 4.606 Ω (the negative root is rejected). (b) The voltage across a shunt is the input of the next section's source: the stage ratio is (voltage of stage n+1)/(voltage of stage n) = (R_x − 2)/R_x = 0.5657, the same at every stage (because 2 + 6∥R_x = R_x, the shunt voltage is the fraction (6∥R_x)/R_x = (R_x − 2)/R_x of the stage input). So the voltage on the third shunt is 10 × 0.5657³ = 1.81 V. (Values along the ladder: 5.66 V, 3.20 V, 1.81 V.) Key insight: self-similarity turns an infinite network into one quadratic. Check: R_x must exceed 2 Ω (series) and be less than 2 + 6 = 8 Ω (if the ladder stopped after one stage); 4.606 fits.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>The six edges of a regular tetrahedron are each a 12 Ω resistor. Find the resistance between two vertices.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Call the vertices A, B (terminals), C, D. By symmetry (mirror exchanging C and D) C and D are at the same potential, so the resistor C–D carries no current: remove it. What is left: the direct A–B resistor (12 Ω), the path A–C–B (24 Ω) and the path A–D–B (24 Ω), all in parallel. 1/R = 1/12 + 1/24 + 1/24 = 4/24, so R = 6 Ω. Key insight: any mirror plane that swaps two other vertices and fixes the terminals puts those vertices at equal potentials. Check: the general rule R_eff = R/2 for K₄; Foster's theorem (Σ over edges of R_eff/R = N − 1 = 3) gives 6 × (6/12) = 3 ✓.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>A cube is made of twelve 1 Ω resistors along its edges. Find the resistance between (a) the two ends of one edge (adjacent corners), (b) opposite corners of one face, (c) opposite corners of the cube. Then use Foster's theorem to get (a) with no circuit reduction at all.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(c) Body diagonal: inject current at corner 0, extract at corner 7. By symmetry the three neighbours of 0 are equipotential and so are the three neighbours of 7. The network is 3 resistors (0 to first layer), 6 resistors (first layer to second layer), 3 resistors (second layer to 7) in series: 1/3 + 1/6 + 1/3 = 5/6 Ω. (b) Face diagonal, from corner 000 to corner 011: the two common neighbours 001 and 010 are equipotential, and so are 101 and 110 (mirror symmetry exchanging y and z). Merge each pair into a node. By the antisymmetry between source and sink, the merged mid nodes are both at half the source voltage, so they can be tied together. The left half (from the source to the mid potential) is then 1/2 (two parallel 1 Ω to the merged 001/010 node) in parallel with [1 Ω to corner 100, then two parallel 1 Ω to the merged 101/110 node = 1 + 1/2]: 1/2 ∥ 3/2 = 3/8. The right half is the same, so R = 3/4 Ω. (a) Edge: the cube is edge-transitive (all 12 edges are equivalent), so Foster's theorem, Σ over edges of R_eff/R_edge = N − 1 = 7, gives 12 × R_edge-pair = 7, i.e. R = 7/12 Ω. Direct check: the edge resistor is in parallel with the rest, and 1 ∥ 7/5 = 7/12. I verified all three results by nodal analysis. Key insight: symmetry reduces the cube to a short chain, and Foster's theorem delivers the edge value in one line. It is also a check for your own results in unfamiliar networks.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>An R-2R ladder: the input node is connected through R = 1.00 kΩ to node 1, then R to node 2, then R to node 3. Each of nodes 1, 2, 3 is connected to ground through a 2R resistor, and node 3 has one extra 2R resistor to ground (the terminator). A 10.0 V source is at the input (relative to ground). (a) Find the input resistance. (b) Find the currents through the three 2R resistors to ground (the terminator is the extra one at node 3). What pattern do you see and why?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) Start from the far end: at node 3 the two 2R resistors in parallel give R. Adding the series R from node 2 gives 2R; in parallel with node 2's own 2R leg gives R. Add the series R from node 1: 2R; in parallel with node 1's 2R leg: R. Add the input R: total 2R = 2.00 kΩ, so I_in = 5.00 mA. (b) Node 1: V₁ = 5.00 mA × R_(looking in to node 1 is R) = 5.00 V; current in its 2R leg = 5/2000 = 2.50 mA, leaving 2.50 mA to continue. Node 2: V₂ = 2.50 V; leg current 1.25 mA. Node 3: V₃ = 1.25 V; the two 2R resistors carry 0.625 mA each. So the currents are 2.50, 1.25, 0.625 mA and 0.625 mA: each one is half the previous, the last two equal. Key insight: at every node, the current sees two equal resistances (2R leg and 2R continuing), so it splits 50:50; that is the binary-weighted property that makes the R-2R digital-to-analog converter work with only two resistor values. Check: 2.5 + 1.25 + 0.625 + 0.625 = 5.00 mA ✓.</div></details>

<h3>Trap list</h3>
<ul>
<li>Adding parallel resistances like series ones, or using R₁R₂/(R₁+R₂) for three resistors (it is for exactly two).</li>
<li>Claiming the voltage divider output is unchanged by a load. It changes unless R_L ≫ R₂.</li>
<li>"Removing a bulb from a parallel set dims the rest." It does not: the remaining branches keep the same voltage. In a <i>series-parallel</i> mix the remaining bulbs can change, as in the A, B, C puzzle.</li>
<li>In a current divider, putting the <i>same</i> resistor in the numerator (I₁ = I R₁/(R₁+R₂)). The other resistor goes on top; the smaller R takes more current.</li>
<li>Assuming a bridge is balanced because it "looks symmetric"; check the ratios R₁/R₂ = R₃/R₄ or verify the symmetry of potential.</li>
<li>Forgetting that a wire makes two points the same node: a drawing can hide parallel resistors.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Teach-back</h3>
<p>Explain to a friend in 3 sentences why the smaller resistor always wins in parallel and the larger one in series, and how symmetry lets you delete a resistor in a bridge or cube.</p>
`;
DATA.chal['c06'] = `
<h3>Why it works</h3>
<p><b>A real source is an ideal source plus a resistor.</b> Chemistry sets the open-circuit voltage ε (energy per charge released by the reaction). But charge must be carried through electrolyte and electrodes, and that has finite conductance, so the cell behaves as if a resistor r were in series inside it. KVL around the loop: ε − Ir − IR = 0, so I = ε/(R + r), and the terminal voltage is V_t = IR = ε − Ir. Units: r has Ω, I·r is volts, consistent. Short-circuit current I_sc = ε/r; open-circuit current is 0. Two measurements (two loads) determine both ε and r, as the puzzle below shows.</p>
<p><b>Maximum power: derivation and meaning.</b> Load power P = I²R = ε²R/(R + r)². At R → 0, P → 0 (current is large but there is no voltage). At R → ∞, P → 0 (voltage is large but no current). In between P peaks: setting dP/dR = 0 gives R = r, P_max = ε²/(4r). At that point half of the power is wasted inside the source: efficiency η = R/(R + r) = 50%.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>R (Ω), ε = 10 V, r = 2 Ω</th><th>I (A)</th><th>P_load (W)</th><th>η</th></tr>
<tr><td>0 (short)</td><td>5.00</td><td>0</td><td>0%</td></tr>
<tr><td>0.5</td><td>4.00</td><td>8.0</td><td>20%</td></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>3.33</td><td>11.1</td><td>33%</td></tr>
<tr><td>2 (matched)</td><td>2.50</td><td>12.5</td><td>50%</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>1.67</td><td>11.1</td><td>67%</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>1.00</td><td>8.0</td><td>80%</td></tr>
<tr><td>∞ (open)</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>100%</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Notice the symmetry: R = 0.5 and R = 8 give the same power, as do 1 and 4. The pairs satisfy R₁R₂ = r² (0.5 × 8 = 4 = 2²), because P(R) is unchanged by R → r²/R. On a log scale the power curve is symmetric about R = r.</p>
<p><b>Combining cells.</b> Series cells: emfs add and internal resistances add. Identical parallel cells: emf is unchanged, r divides by n. For a given load R, the best arrangement of a fixed number of cells puts the pack's internal resistance near R (matching), when maximum power is the goal; when efficiency or runtime matters, you want r ≪ R instead.</p>
<p><b>Energy and runtime.</b> Energy = V × charge capacity: E (Wh) = V × Ah. A 3.7 V, 2.0 Ah cell holds 7.4 Wh = 26.6 kJ (×3600). Runtime ≈ capacity/current. Units: A·h × V = W·h.</p>

<h3>Tools of thought</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Model first.</b> Replace every real source by (ε, r). Short-circuit current and terminal voltage then follow from one series loop.</li>
<li><b>Two-point identification.</b> Two load measurements give two equations: ε = V₁ + (V₁/R₁)r = V₂ + (V₂/R₂)r. Subtract to get r.</li>
<li><b>Parallel EMFs: use the two-node formula.</b> For cells in parallel with a load, V = (Σ ε_i/r_i)/(Σ 1/r_i + 1/R). This is nodal analysis on one node.</li>
<li><b>Matching versus efficiency.</b> Ask what you are optimizing: power to the load (match R = r, η = 50%) or efficiency (R ≫ r). Power lines and batteries for runtime go for efficiency; speakers and radios for power.</li>
<li><b>Order-of-magnitude runtime.</b> Energy/power. A 10 Wh pack driving a 2 W load lasts about 5 h before derating.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Challenge problems</h3>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>A weak, cold 12 V car battery has internal resistance 0.020 Ω. A starter motor draws 200 A. Find the terminal voltage and the power wasted inside the battery.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>V_t = ε − Ir = 12 − 200 × 0.020 = 8.0 V. Power wasted inside: I²r = 200² × 0.020 = 800 W. Power delivered to the starter: V_t I = 8.0 × 200 = 1600 W. Total = εI = 2400 W ✓. Key insight: high current through even a small r gives a large drop and a lot of internal heating. Check: efficiency = 1600/2400 = 67%; a healthy battery (r of a few mΩ) would show V_t above 11 V at 200 A.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>Three AA cells (each 1.5 V, 0.20 Ω internal) are connected in series to a 4.2 Ω load. Find the current, the load power, and the power lost inside the cells.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>ε_tot = 4.5 V, r_tot = 0.60 Ω. I = 4.5/(0.60 + 4.2) = 0.9375 A. P_load = I²R = 0.9375² × 4.2 = 3.69 W. P_internal = I²r = 0.527 W. Total supplied εI = 4.22 W ✓ (3.69 + 0.53). Terminal voltage = 4.5 − 0.9375 × 0.6 = 3.94 V. Key insight: series cells add both ε and r, so the efficiency is R/(R + r) = 4.2/4.8 = 87.5%. Check: 3.69/4.22 = 0.875 ✓.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>A source has ε = 10 V and r = 2.0 Ω. (a) What load R gives maximum power and what is that power? (b) Show that loads of 0.50 Ω and 8.0 Ω dissipate the same power, and explain. (c) Which would you choose for a long battery life?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) R = r = 2.0 Ω, P_max = ε²/(4r) = 100/8 = 12.5 W. (b) R = 0.5: I = 10/2.5 = 4.0 A, P = 16 × 0.5 = 8.0 W. R = 8: I = 10/10 = 1.0 A, P = 1 × 8 = 8.0 W. Explanation: P(R) = ε²R/(R+r)² is invariant under R → r²/R (check: substituting gives ε²(r²/R)/((r²+rR)²/R²) = ε²R r²/(r²(r+R)²)). Here 0.5 × 8 = 4 = r². (c) The 8 Ω load: same power as 0.5 Ω but with efficiency 80% instead of 20%. Key insight: for a given power level there are two loads, and the larger R wastes far less in the battery. Check: in case (b) total energy drawn: εI = 40 W vs 10 W.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>Two cells are connected in parallel: cell 1 has ε = 1.50 V, r = 0.50 Ω and cell 2 has ε = 1.30 V, r = 0.30 Ω. (a) With no load, what current circulates and what is the terminal voltage? (b) With a 1.0 Ω load, find the terminal voltage and the current in each cell.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) The loop contains both cells, and their emfs oppose: I = (1.50 − 1.30)/(0.50 + 0.30) = 0.25 A, flowing out of cell 1 and into cell 2 (cell 2 is being charged). Terminal voltage: 1.50 − 0.25 × 0.50 = 1.375 V (check with cell 2: 1.30 + 0.25 × 0.30 = 1.375 V ✓). Wasted power: I²(0.8) = 0.05 W. (b) With the 1 Ω load, V = (1.5/0.5 + 1.3/0.3)/(1/0.5 + 1/0.3 + 1/1) = (3 + 4.333)/(2 + 3.333 + 1) = 1.158 V. Currents: cell 1: (1.5 − 1.158)/0.5 = 0.684 A; cell 2: (1.3 − 1.158)/0.3 = 0.474 A; load: 1.158 A = 0.684 + 0.474 ✓. Key insight: paralleling cells of different emf wastes energy in circulating current; this is why you never mix old and new cells in parallel. Check: the loaded terminal voltage 1.158 V lies below the lower emf 1.30 V, so both cells supply current.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>A mystery battery is measured. Across a 10.0 Ω resistor it gives 8.00 V; across a 4.00 Ω resistor it gives 6.40 V. Find ε and r. What is the maximum power the battery could deliver, and to what load?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Load currents: 0.800 A and 1.60 A. Write ε = V + Ir for both: ε = 8.00 + 0.800r = 6.40 + 1.60r. Subtract: 1.60 = 0.800r, so r = 2.0 Ω, and ε = 8.00 + 1.60 = 9.6 V. Max power at R = r = 2.0 Ω: P = ε²/(4r) = 92.16/8 = 11.5 W. Key insight: you never need to open the box; two loads give two equations. Check: at 4 Ω: I = 9.6/6 = 1.6 A, V = 6.4 V ✓; at 10 Ω: I = 9.6/12 = 0.8 A, V = 8.0 V ✓. (The two measured loads dissipated 6.4 W and 10.2 W; the matched 2 Ω load would get 11.5 W.)</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>You have six identical cells, each 1.5 V with 1.0 Ω internal resistance, and a fixed 1.5 Ω load. You can wire them as 6 in series, 3 series-strings of 2 in parallel (written "3S × 2P", meaning 3 cells in series in each of 2 parallel strings), 2 in series × 3 strings, or all 6 in parallel. Which arrangement delivers the most power to the load, and how much?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>A pack of s cells in series and p parallel strings has ε = 1.5s and r = s/p Ω. The options: 6S1P: ε = 9, r = 6 Ω → I = 9/7.5 = 1.20 A, P = 1.44 × 1.5 = 2.16 W. 3S2P: ε = 4.5, r = 1.5 Ω → I = 4.5/3 = 1.50 A, P = 3.375 W. 2S3P: ε = 3, r = 0.667 → I = 3/2.167 = 1.385 A, P = 2.88 W. 1S6P: ε = 1.5, r = 0.167 → I = 0.90 A, P = 1.215 W. The best is 3S2P, 3.375 W, where r_pack = 1.5 Ω equals the load. Key insight: this is maximum power transfer in disguise; the series/parallel count is a transformer for both ε and r. With s × p = 6 fixed, r_pack = s/p, so matching r_pack = 1.5 Ω means s/p = 1.5, i.e. s = 3, p = 2. Check: P_max = ε²/4r = 20.25/6 = 3.375 ✓.</div></details>

<h3>Trap list</h3>
<ul>
<li>Assuming terminal voltage equals the EMF under load. It equals ε only with no current. A "dead" battery may read full voltage on a meter (which draws nothing) and collapse under load.</li>
<li>Thinking maximum power transfer means maximum efficiency. At R = r the efficiency is only 50%.</li>
<li>Adding voltages of parallel cells (parallel keeps ε, adds capacity) or capacities of series cells (series adds ε, keeps capacity).</li>
<li>Mixing old and new cells in series or parallel. The weak cell limits current (series) or gets driven backwards (parallel).</li>
<li>Energy units: Wh vs Ah (V × Ah = Wh) and mAh to Ah (÷1000).</li>
<li>Reading "short-circuit current" as the test current: measuring it on a real cell is dangerous because ε/r can be tens or hundreds of amps.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Teach-back</h3>
<p>Explain to a friend in 3 sentences why a battery's voltage sags under load (internal resistance, V_t = ε − Ir) and why the load that extracts the most power only gets half of the total.</p>
`;
DATA.chal['c07'] = `
<h3>Why it works</h3>
<p><b>KCL is conservation of charge.</b> In steady state charge cannot pile up at a node, so what flows in flows out. Nothing about the elements matters: it holds for resistors, batteries, diodes or anything else. <b>KVL is conservation of energy</b>: the electrostatic field is conservative, so the work per unit charge around any closed path is zero. (With a changing magnetic flux through the loop it needs an extra term, which is exactly why inductors appear as "voltage elements" in the general law.) Units: amperes add, volts add; you can never add an amp to a volt, which is a quick check of a wrongly assembled equation.</p>
<p><b>Counting equations tells you which method to use.</b> A network with b branches and n nodes has b unknown branch currents, n − 1 independent KCL equations, and b − n + 1 independent KVL equations (for a planar circuit: the number of windows). Nodal analysis needs n − 1 unknown voltages <i>minus</i> the number of voltage sources (each fixes a voltage difference). Mesh analysis needs the number of windows minus the number of current sources. Pick the smaller system: a circuit with 3 nodes and 5 windows is a nodal problem; a circuit with 6 nodes and 2 windows is a mesh problem.</p>
<p><b>Why the linear system has exactly one solution.</b> For resistors and independent sources, the equations are linear and the conductance matrix is positive definite once a reference node is chosen: any steady state with zero sources and no current would need zero dissipation, so all currents are zero. So if you find <i>a</i> solution that satisfies KCL, KVL and Ohm, it is <i>the</i> solution.</p>
<p><b>Power balance is a free checksum (Tellegen).</b> Over a whole network the sum of v·i for all elements (with consistent sign convention) is zero: power delivered by sources equals power absorbed. A battery that is being charged absorbs power (current enters +) and so counts on the "absorbed" side. It also justifies superposition only for <i>voltages and currents</i>: power is quadratic, so P_total ≠ P₁ + P₂ in general.</p>
<p><b>Reciprocity.</b> In a network of resistors and independent sources, if an ideal voltage source in branch A produces current I in an ammeter in branch B, then moving the same source into B and the ammeter into A gives the same I. (Verified numerically on a random 5-node network below; it follows from the symmetry of the conductance matrix.) It fails for networks with dependent sources or diodes.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Method</th><th>Unknown</th><th>Use when</th><th>Special case</th></tr>
<tr><td>Branch currents</td><td>b currents</td><td>tiny circuits</td><td>–</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nodal</td><td>n − 1 node voltages</td><td>few nodes, many loops, current sources</td><td>voltage source between nodes → supernode</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mesh</td><td>window currents</td><td>few windows, voltage sources</td><td>current source shared by meshes → supermesh</td></tr>
<tr><td>Superposition</td><td>one source at a time</td><td>few sources, want contribution of each</td><td>off = short (V), open (I); keep dependent sources on</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Tools of thought</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Choose the reference node with the most connections</b> (usually the bottom rail). That makes most node voltages "free" and most equations short.</li>
<li><b>Two-node shortcut (Millman).</b> For any number of branches (ε_i with series R_i) between just two nodes, V = (Σ ε_i/R_i)/(Σ 1/R_i). Seconds, not minutes.</li>
<li><b>Count before you write.</b> Decide nodal versus mesh by counting unknowns, then look at what each source type does to the count.</li>
<li><b>Guess any direction.</b> A wrong guess gives a negative current; it never spoils the solution. A negative power on a battery means it is being charged.</li>
<li><b>Sanity-check with power balance or an unused loop.</b> After solving, check Σ P_source = Σ I²R, or apply KVL around a loop you did not use.</li>
<li><b>Symmetry and reciprocity.</b> If the circuit looks the same viewed from two terminals, currents or voltages are related without computation.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Challenge problems</h3>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>Three branches meet at a top node and all return to a common bottom rail. Branch 1: a 20 V battery in series with 5 Ω. Branch 2: a 10 Ω resistor. Branch 3: an 8 V battery in series with 2 Ω (both batteries have their + terminals toward the top node). Find the top-node voltage, each branch current, and state which elements deliver or absorb power.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Two-node formula: V = (20/5 + 8/2)/(1/5 + 1/10 + 1/2) = 8/0.8 = 10.0 V. Branch currents (into the node positive): branch 1 = (20 − 10)/5 = 2.0 A into the node; branch 2 = 10/10 = 1.0 A out to ground; branch 3 = (8 − 10)/2 = −1.0 A, meaning 1.0 A flows <i>into</i> the 8 V battery's + terminal, so it is being charged. KCL: 2.0 − 1.0 (to the 10 Ω) − 1.0 (into battery 3) = 0 ✓. Powers: battery 1 delivers 20 × 2 = 40 W; battery 3 absorbs 8 × 1 = 8 W. Dissipation: 5 Ω: 4 × 5 = 20 W; 2 Ω: 1 × 2 = 2 W; 10 Ω: 10 W. Total dissipated 32 W = 40 − 8 ✓. Key insight: the battery "sign" is decided by the current direction, not by its label.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>Mesh analysis. Mesh 1 (left window) has a 12 V source and a 3 Ω resistor, and shares a 6 Ω resistor with mesh 2. Mesh 2 (right window) has a 4 Ω resistor and a 6 V battery that opposes the clockwise current. Find the two clockwise mesh currents and the current in the shared 6 Ω resistor.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Mesh 1: (3 + 6)I₁ − 6I₂ = 12. Mesh 2: −6I₁ + (6 + 4)I₂ = −6 (the battery opposes clockwise flow). From the first: I₁ = (12 + 6I₂)/9. Substituting: −6(12 + 6I₂)/9 + 10I₂ = −6 → −8 − 4I₂ + 10I₂ = −6 → 6I₂ = 2 → I₂ = 0.333 A, I₁ = 1.556 A. The shared 6 Ω carries I₁ − I₂ = 1.222 A (in mesh 1's direction). Key insight: the shared resistor gets the <i>difference</i> of mesh currents. Check (power balance): the 12 V source delivers 12 × 1.556 = 18.67 W; the 6 V battery absorbs 6 × 0.333 = 2.0 W because I₂ flows against its emf (it is being charged). Dissipation: 3 × 1.556² + 6 × 1.222² + 4 × 0.333² = 7.26 + 8.96 + 0.44 = 16.67 W = 18.67 − 2.0 ✓.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>A 24 V source feeds node 1 through a 4 Ω resistor. Node 1 connects to ground through 6 Ω. Between node 1 and node 2 there is an ideal 10 V battery (node 2 is 10 V above node 1). Node 2 connects to ground through 3 Ω. Find all node voltages and the current through the floating battery.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Nodes 1 and 2 form a supernode (the battery fixes V₂ = V₁ + 10). KCL for the supernode: current in from the source = current out through both resistors: (24 − V₁)/4 = V₁/6 + V₂/3. Substitute V₂: 6 − V₁/4 = V₁/6 + (V₁ + 10)/3 → 6 − 10/3 = V₁(1/4 + 1/6 + 1/3) → 2.667 = 0.75V₁ → V₁ = 3.556 V, V₂ = 13.556 V. Currents: through the 4 Ω: (24 − 3.556)/4 = 5.111 A; through 6 Ω: 0.593 A; through 3 Ω: 4.519 A. The battery current is the current into node 2's resistor, 4.519 A, from node 1 into the battery's − terminal and out the + terminal (it is delivering 10 × 4.519 = 45.2 W). Key insight: the battery's current is not given by Ohm's law; you read it from KCL at one of its terminals. Check: 0.593 + 4.519 = 5.111 ✓.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>A bridge is fed by 12.0 V. The left arm has 100 Ω (top) and 200 Ω (bottom); the right arm has 300 Ω (top) and 100 Ω (bottom); a 50 Ω resistor joins the two midpoints. Find the current in the 50 Ω and its direction.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Bottom rail = 0 V. Left midpoint P, right midpoint Q. KCL at P: (P − 12)/100 + P/200 + (P − Q)/50 = 0 → 0.035P − 0.02Q = 0.12. KCL at Q: (Q − 12)/300 + Q/100 + (Q − P)/50 = 0 → −0.02P + 0.03333Q = 0.04. Solving: P = 6.261 V, Q = 4.957 V. The current in the 50 Ω is (6.261 − 4.957)/50 = 26.1 mA from P to Q. Key insight: an unbalanced bridge is a two-unknown nodal problem; check balance first, since 100/200 ≠ 300/100 (ratios 0.5 and 3), so the bridge is very unbalanced. Check: currents into P: (12 − 6.261)/100 = 57.4 mA; out through 200 Ω: 31.3 mA; out through 50 Ω: 26.1 mA ✓ (57.4 = 31.3 + 26.1).</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>Two sources act on a 1 Ω resistor. Alone, source A would drive 2 A through it; alone, source B would drive 3 A through it in the same direction. With both on, what is the power in the resistor? What if B's polarity is reversed? What does "adding the two single-source powers" give, and why is it wrong?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Currents superpose: I = 2 + 3 = 5 A, so P = I²R = 25 W. With B reversed: I = 2 − 3 = −1 A, so P = 1 W. Adding the single-source powers gives 4 + 9 = 13 W, which matches neither. Reason: P = I²R is quadratic, (I_A + I_B)² = I_A² + I_B² + 2I_AI_B; the cross term 2I_AI_B = ±12 W is the interaction. Key insight: superposition is a theorem for linear quantities (V, I), never for power (or energy). Check: 13 + 12 = 25 and 13 − 12 = 1 ✓.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>A resistor network is a sealed box with terminals A and B (branch 1) and terminals C and D (branch 2). An ideal 12 V source across A–B makes 3.0 mA flow through an ideal ammeter connected across C–D. The source and ammeter are swapped (source across C–D, ammeter across A–B, same orientation of the terminals). What does the ammeter read? What if the source is 30 V?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Reciprocity: the ammeter reads 3.0 mA again. With 30 V: current scales linearly, 3.0 × 30/12 = 7.5 mA. Key insight: for a network of resistors only, the transfer conductance "current at 2 per volt at 1" equals "current at 1 per volt at 2", because the conductance matrix is symmetric. I verified the reciprocity numerically on a random 5-node, 8-resistor network (both readings matched to seven digits). Check: units: 3.0 mA/12 V = 0.25 mS of transfer conductance; it does not matter which way you use it. Warning: it does <i>not</i> hold if the box contains a dependent source, a diode or a transistor.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>Two ideal batteries, 12.0 V and 11.9 V, are connected in parallel with no load. (a) What does KVL predict if both are ideal? (b) Two real batteries have internal resistances 0.10 Ω and 0.05 Ω. What current circulates and what power is wasted?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) Around the loop KVL gives 12.0 − 11.9 = 0.1 V dropped across zero resistance: I = 0.1 V/0 Ω, which is impossible. The model is inconsistent: ideal sources of different emf cannot be connected in parallel. (b) With internal resistances: I = (12.0 − 11.9)/(0.10 + 0.05) = 0.667 A circulating, from the higher battery into the lower (charging it). Wasted power: I²(r₁ + r₂) = 0.667² × 0.15 = 0.0667 W. Key insight: a contradiction from KVL means the model is missing a resistance, so the real circulating current is set by the small internal resistances and is huge relative to the emf difference (here 0.667 A for a 0.1 V mismatch). Check: 0.667 × 0.1 = 0.0667 W ✓ (the emf difference times current).</div></details>

<h3>Trap list</h3>
<ul>
<li>Sign errors in KVL: write the direction of travel first and then +ε for − to + crossings and −IR when going along the current.</li>
<li>Assuming the battery always delivers power. Current into the + terminal means charging (it absorbs power).</li>
<li>Mixing loop current and branch current in a shared resistor (it carries the <i>difference</i> I₁ − I₂ of the mesh currents).</li>
<li>Turning off dependent sources in superposition (they are part of the circuit's "resistance"; leave them on).</li>
<li>Applying superposition to power: P is not additive.</li>
<li>A floating voltage source has no Ohm's-law current: use a supernode (KCL around both nodes) rather than trying to divide V by a resistance.</li>
<li>Connecting ideal sources of different emf in parallel, or ideal current sources in series with different values: inconsistent models, not an engineering result.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Teach-back</h3>
<p>Explain to a friend in 3 sentences why KCL is conservation of charge, why KVL is conservation of energy, and how you decide between nodal and mesh by counting unknowns.</p>
`;
DATA.chal['c08'] = `
<h3>Why it works</h3>
<p><b>A linear two-terminal network has a straight-line V–I curve.</b> Because every element is linear, the terminal voltage must be an affine function of the terminal current: V = V_th − R_th·I. A straight line is fixed by any two points, and two are easy: the <i>open-circuit</i> point (I = 0, V = V_th) and the <i>short-circuit</i> point (V = 0, I = I_sc = V_th/R_th). That is the whole content of Thévenin and Norton: the same line read in two ways, as a voltage source V_th in series with R_th, or as a current source I_N = V_th/R_th in parallel with R_N = R_th. Units: R_th = V_th/I_sc has units V/A = Ω.</p>
<p><b>Why "zero the sources" gives R_th.</b> The slope of the V–I line is the same if you turn all the independent sources off, because the line is shifted but not tilted by the independent sources. With the sources off, the slope is just the resistance looking into the terminals. With <i>dependent</i> sources you cannot turn them off (they respond to what the network does), so use R_th = V_oc/I_sc, or drive the port with a test source and compute v_test/i_test.</p>
<p><b>Maximum power is a parabola.</b> With R_L across a Thévenin source, P = V_th²R_L/(R_L + R_th)², maximum at R_L = R_th where P = V_th²/(4R_th) and the efficiency (in the source network) is 50%. Important: this is maximum power for a <i>variable load</i>. If the load is fixed and you can vary the source side, you want R_th as small as possible.</p>
<p><b>Wheatstone bridge.</b> Each arm pair is a divider. The midpoints are at V_s R₂/(R₁+R₂) and V_s R_x/(R₃+R_x). They are equal, and the detector reads zero, when R₂/(R₁+R₂) = R_x/(R₃+R_x), i.e. R₁/R₂ = R₃/R_x. Near balance, a small change ΔR in one arm of an R-valued bridge gives V_out ≈ V_s ΔR/(4R): the output is linear in ΔR, and proportional to V_s, which is why strain-gauge and RTD sensors are read with bridges. Making two arms active doubles the output; four appropriately arranged active arms quadruple it.</p>
<p><b>Δ–Y equivalence.</b> Two three-terminal networks are equivalent if the resistance between every pair of terminals matches. For the special case of equal legs: between two terminals the Δ gives R_Δ ∥ 2R_Δ = 2R_Δ/3, and the Y gives 2R_Y, so R_Y = R_Δ/3. In general, R_Y(at A) = R_AB·R_AC/(R_AB + R_BC + R_CA): product of the two Δ legs touching A over the sum of all three. The benefit: a Δ or Y hiding inside a bridge turns the circuit into plain series-parallel.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Network</th><th>Thévenin</th><th>Norton</th></tr>
<tr><td>source</td><td>V_th in series with R_th</td><td>I_N = V_th/R_th in parallel with R_N = R_th</td></tr>
<tr><td>open-circuit voltage</td><td>V_th</td><td>I_N·R_N</td></tr>
<tr><td>short-circuit current</td><td>V_th/R_th</td><td>I_N</td></tr>
<tr><td>internal dissipation with the terminals open</td><td>0</td><td>I_N²R_N (so the two differ inside; only the terminal behaviour matches)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Tools of thought</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Take out the thing you care about.</b> Remove the load (or the detector), find V_th and R_th of the rest, then reattach. Especially good for "current in one branch for several values of a resistor".</li>
<li><b>Source transformation.</b> A voltage source V in series with R equals a current source V/R in parallel with R. Convert, combine parallel current sources and parallel resistors, convert back: a ladder of reductions with no equations.</li>
<li><b>V_oc/I_sc for anything with dependent sources.</b> Two easy circuits (open and short), then divide.</li>
<li><b>Balance first.</b> Check R₁/R₂ = R₃/R₄ before you start on a bridge. If balanced, the detector branch vanishes and the rest is series-parallel.</li>
<li><b>Δ–Y for unbalanced bridges.</b> Convert one triangle to a star and finish with series-parallel; or use Thévenin across the detector. Choose whichever needs fewer steps.</li>
<li><b>Fixed load versus fixed source.</b> Ask which side you control before invoking "match the resistances".</li>
</ul>

<h3>Challenge problems</h3>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>A 24 V source is in series with 6.0 Ω. The node after the 6.0 Ω connects through a 12 Ω resistor to ground. The output terminals are across the 12 Ω resistor. Find the Thévenin and Norton equivalents. What current and power does an 8.0 Ω load draw? What load would get the maximum power, and how much?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>V_th = 24 × 12/(6 + 12) = 16 V. R_th = 6 ∥ 12 = 4.0 Ω. Norton: I_N = V_th/R_th = 4.0 A in parallel with 4.0 Ω. An 8.0 Ω load: I = 16/(4 + 8) = 1.333 A, V_L = 10.67 V, P = I²R = 14.2 W. Maximum power: R_L = R_th = 4.0 Ω, P_max = V_th²/(4R_th) = 256/16 = 16 W (I = 2 A). Key insight: the network's complexity disappears into two numbers. Check: short-circuit current directly: 24/6 = 4.0 A, matching I_N.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>A 10 V supply feeds a divider of two 1.0 MΩ resistors in series. A digital voltmeter with 10 MΩ input resistance is connected across the bottom resistor. What does it read, and what is the percentage error?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Thévenin of the divider at the measurement terminals: V_th = 5.0 V, R_th = 1 ∥ 1 = 0.5 MΩ. The meter is a 10 MΩ load: V_meter = 5.0 × 10/(10 + 0.5) = 4.762 V. Error = (4.762 − 5.0)/5.0 = −4.8%. Key insight: a meter is just a load. The measurement is accurate only when R_meter ≫ R_th (here 20 times larger gives 4.8% error, consistent with R_th/(R_th + R_meter) = 0.5/10.5). Check: the simple divider with the meter in parallel: 1 ∥ 10 = 0.909 MΩ; 10 × 0.909/(1 + 0.909) = 4.76 V ✓.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>A bridge is fed by 12.0 V. Left arm: 100 Ω (top), 200 Ω (bottom). Right arm: 150 Ω (top), 100 Ω (bottom). A galvanometer of resistance 50.0 Ω joins the midpoints. Find the galvanometer current by Thévenin's theorem.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Remove the galvanometer. Left midpoint voltage (relative to the bottom) = 12 × 200/300 = 8.000 V; right midpoint = 12 × 100/250 = 4.800 V. V_th = 3.200 V. R_th: with the source shorted, looking into the two midpoints we see (100 ∥ 200) in series with (150 ∥ 100) = 66.67 + 60.0 = 126.67 Ω. Galvanometer current: I = 3.200/(126.67 + 50) = 18.1 mA, from the left midpoint to the right. Key insight: the detector is the "load"; everything else is a two-terminal network. Check: full nodal analysis gives P = 6.792 V, Q = 5.887 V, (P − Q)/50 = 18.1 mA ✓. Balanced condition would need 100/200 = 150/100; it is not.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>A bridge has a source (terminal S, top) and a return terminal D (bottom). Arms: S–P = 6.0 Ω, S–Q = 3.0 Ω, P–D = 4.5 Ω, Q–D = 5.25 Ω, and a bridging resistor P–Q = 3.0 Ω. Find the resistance between S and D using a Δ–Y transformation.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>The triangle S–P–Q has legs R_SP = 6, R_SQ = 3, R_PQ = 3, sum 12 Ω. Convert to a star with centre O: R_S = (6 × 3)/12 = 1.5 Ω; R_P = (6 × 3)/12 = 1.5 Ω; R_Q = (3 × 3)/12 = 0.75 Ω. Now the path from O to D has two branches: O–P then P–D: 1.5 + 4.5 = 6 Ω; O–Q then Q–D: 0.75 + 5.25 = 6 Ω. In parallel: 6 ∥ 6 = 3 Ω. Total: 1.5 + 3 = 4.5 Ω. Key insight: each star resistor is (product of the two triangle legs touching that terminal)/(sum of legs). Check: nodal analysis on the original network also gives 4.50 Ω. The bridge is unbalanced (6/3 = 2 but 4.5/5.25 = 0.857), so the bridging resistor does matter, and simple series-parallel reduction fails without Δ–Y. Bounds: removing the bridging resistor gives (6 + 4.5) ∥ (3 + 5.25) = 4.62 Ω and shorting it gives (6 ∥ 3) + (4.5 ∥ 5.25) = 4.42 Ω, so 4.5 Ω lies between them as it must.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>Terminals a and b (b is ground). A 10 V source with 5.0 Ω in series feeds node a. A 20 Ω resistor connects a to ground. A dependent current source injects 0.030·v_a amperes into node a (v_a in volts; it is proportional to the voltage at a). Find the Thévenin equivalent at a–b. A classmate "turns off" the dependent source to find R_th. What does that give, and why is it wrong?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Open circuit: KCL at a: (10 − v)/5 + 0.030v = v/20 → 2 − 0.2v + 0.03v = 0.05v → 2 = 0.22v → V_th = v = 9.091 V. Short circuit (v_a = 0): the 20 Ω carries nothing, the dependent source produces 0.03 × 0 = 0, so I_sc = 10/5 = 2.0 A. R_th = V_oc/I_sc = 9.091/2.0 = 4.545 Ω. (Test-source check: turn the 10 V off, inject 1 A at a: v(1/5 + 1/20 − 0.03) = 1 → v = 1/0.22 = 4.545 V ✓.) The classmate's shortcut: 5 ∥ 20 = 4.0 Ω, which is wrong because the dependent source depends on v_a, so it acts like extra (negative) conductance −0.030 S: it "pushes back" and raises R_th. Key insight: with controlled sources, R_th comes from V_oc/I_sc or a test source. Check: the 12% difference (4.0 against 4.545 Ω) grows as the coefficient grows: at 0.25 S the net conductance 0.2 + 0.05 − 0.25 reaches zero and R_th becomes infinite.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>A strain-gauge bridge has four 350 Ω arms excited by 5.00 V. One gauge (an "active arm") increases by 0.10% (0.35 Ω). (a) Find the output voltage (between midpoints). (b) Compare with the small-signal formula V_s ΔR/(4R). (c) What if two gauges are active, arranged so their effects add (one +0.35 Ω, one −0.35 Ω in the right arms), or all four?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) Output = 5[(R + ΔR)/(2R + ΔR) − 1/2] = 5 × (350.35/700.35 − 0.5) = 5 × 0.000250 = 1.2494 mV. (b) V_s ΔR/(4R) = 5 × 0.35/1400 = 1.2500 mV. The formula is accurate to 0.05%. (c) Two active arms (one rising, one falling, in arms where the effects add): V_s ΔR/(2R) = 2.50 mV. Four active arms: V_s ΔR/R = 5.00 mV. Key insight: a ΔR of 0.1% produces about 0.025% of the excitation, so the output is microvolt-to-millivolt: hence amplifiers and instrumentation. The linearity (V_out ∝ ΔR) holds for small ΔR/R; the exact expression is ΔR/(2(2R + ΔR)) × V_s. Check: 0.35/(2 × 700.35) × 5 = 1.2494 mV ✓.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>A fixed 4.0 Ω load is fed from a 12 V source. You may insert an adjustable series resistor r ≥ 0 between the source and the load. What r maximizes power in the load? What if the load is adjustable and r = 4.0 Ω is fixed? Give both powers.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Fixed load, adjustable r: P_L = (12/(4 + r))² × 4 is largest when r = 0: 36 W (r = 1: 23.0 W; r = 4: 9.0 W). The maximum-power theorem does not apply because the load, not the source resistance, is fixed: extra series resistance only wastes voltage. Adjustable load, fixed r = 4.0 Ω: R_L = r = 4.0 Ω gives P = 12²/(4 × 4) = 9.0 W. Key insight: "match the resistances" is only for a fixed source and a variable load. Check: 36 W with r = 0 is four times the matched 9.0 W, which shows how much the choice of what to vary matters.</div></details>

<h3>Trap list</h3>
<ul>
<li>Zeroing dependent sources when finding R_th. Only <i>independent</i> sources are turned off.</li>
<li>Shorting a current source or opening a voltage source by mistake: voltage sources → short, current sources → open.</li>
<li>Using the maximum-power theorem for a fixed load (it only applies to a variable load on a fixed source); and thinking it gives maximum efficiency (it gives 50%).</li>
<li>Mixing up Δ–Y formulas: Δ→Y puts the <i>sum</i> in the denominator (product of adjacent legs over the sum); Y→Δ puts the <i>opposite</i> Y leg in the denominator (pair products over the opposite leg).</li>
<li>Forgetting that a balanced bridge means the detector branch has zero current, so its resistance is irrelevant; and calling a bridge balanced because it "looks symmetric".</li>
<li>Forgetting the meter as a load: a 10 MΩ voltmeter across a megohm divider reads noticeably low.</li>
<li>Thévenin equivalent only reproduces the terminals' behaviour, not the power inside: the power dissipated in R_th is not the power dissipated in the original network.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Teach-back</h3>
<p>Explain to a friend in 3 sentences why any linear circuit seen from two terminals looks like a battery with a resistor, and how you find the two numbers from an open-circuit and a short-circuit test.</p>
`;
DATA.chal['c09'] = `
<h3>Why it works</h3>
<p><b>Most DC hazards are Ohm's law and I²R in disguise.</b> A voltage by itself injures no one; it is the current it drives through the body (I = V/R_body) and the heat it produces (P = I²R) that matter. R_body depends strongly on the path and on the skin: dry skin is about 100 kΩ to 1 MΩ, wet skin about 1 kΩ, and the body interior a few hundred ohms. So the same 120 V can produce about 1 mA (dry, high-resistance contact) or about 120 mA (wet skin). Units check: V/Ω = A; then mA for body effects.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Situation</th><th>V</th><th>R_body (assumed)</th><th>I = V/R</th><th>Effect level (see lesson)</th></tr>
<tr><td>9 V battery, dry hands</td><td>9</td><td>100 kΩ</td><td>0.09 mA</td><td>below 1 mA perception</td></tr>
<tr><td>12 V car battery, dry</td><td>12</td><td>100 kΩ</td><td>0.12 mA</td><td>imperceptible</td></tr>
<tr><td>120 V, dry</td><td>120</td><td>100 kΩ</td><td>1.2 mA</td><td>about the perception threshold</td></tr>
<tr><td>120 V, wet</td><td>120</td><td>1 kΩ</td><td>120 mA</td><td>in the fibrillation range</td></tr>
<tr><td>12 V, wet</td><td>12</td><td>1 kΩ</td><td>12 mA</td><td>around the let-go threshold</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Short circuits: who limits the current?</b> I_sc = ε/(r + R_short). For a car battery (ε = 12 V, r ≈ 24 mΩ) bridged by a ring (R ≈ 5 mΩ): I = 12/0.029 = 414 A. The ring dissipates I²R = 856 W, while the battery's own internal resistance dissipates 4.1 kW. The danger is not the 12 V; it is the small resistance that allows a huge current, and a small conductor with low heat capacity takes all of that power.</p>
<p><b>Heating at a bad connection.</b> A series chain carries the same I everywhere, so the heat in each element is proportional to its R: P = I²R. A loose joint of 1 Ω in a 15 A circuit generates 225 W at one tiny spot, versus 2.25 W for a good 10 mΩ joint. The breaker does not notice: the current is still the legal 15 A. That is why loose terminals start fires and why arc-fault protection exists.</p>
<p><b>Capacitors are batteries that do not advertise.</b> Stored energy U = ½CV². Units: F·V² = J. A charged capacitor can deliver its charge long after the power is removed, and the decay is exponential with τ = R_path·C, so the danger persists for several τ even when the source is unplugged.</p>
<p><b>Why DC and AC differ for the body (lesson-level facts).</b> A steady DC current tends to cause one sharp muscle contraction at make and break; 50 to 60 Hz AC can hold muscles in tetany so the victim cannot let go (let-go threshold about 10 mA) and is also especially effective at disturbing the heart. DC is not safe at high voltage: it burns, causes electrolytic effects, and can cause fatal injury.</p>

<h3>Tools of thought</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Estimate current with round numbers.</b> Dry ≈ 100 kΩ, wet ≈ 1 kΩ. Divide the voltage; compare with 1 mA (perceptible), 10 mA (let-go), 100 mA (fibrillation range).</li>
<li><b>Ask "what limits the current?"</b> The hazard is large when nothing limits it (a short circuit) and small when a large R does. The safety device must be sized to the wire, not to the load.</li>
<li><b>Heating scales as I²R for the same current, V²/R for the same voltage.</b> Choose by what is fixed. A poor contact (fixed current) hurts; a low-R short on a voltage source (fixed voltage) hurts more.</li>
<li><b>Energy and time constants for capacitors.</b> ½CV² for the energy; τ = RC for the time; for safety, a bleeder resistor drains it in about 5τ.</li>
<li><b>Potential difference, not potential.</b> A bird on a high-voltage wire is safe because it touches one conductor at nearly one potential; the dangerous thing is a path between two different potentials.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Challenge problems</h3>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>Estimate the body current for (a) touching the terminals of a 12 V battery with dry hands (100 kΩ), and (b) touching a 120 V hot wire with wet hands (1 kΩ). Compare them and say which factor (V or R) contributes more to the difference.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) I = 12/100 000 = 0.12 mA. (b) I = 120/1000 = 120 mA. The ratio is 1000: voltage contributes a factor 10 (120/12), resistance a factor 100 (100 kΩ/1 kΩ). Key insight: the skin state matters more than the voltage in this comparison. (a) is below the ~1 mA perception threshold; (b) is in the ventricular-fibrillation range of the lesson (75 to 250 mA). Check: 0.12 mA × 1000 = 120 mA ✓.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>A metal ring (5.0 mΩ) accidentally bridges the terminals of a car battery (ε = 12 V, r = 24 mΩ). Find the current, the power in the ring and in the battery, and estimate how fast a 5 g gold ring would heat (c ≈ 130 J/(kg·K)), ignoring heat loss.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>I = 12/(0.024 + 0.005) = 414 A. Ring power: I²R = 414² × 0.005 = 856 W. Battery internal: 414² × 0.024 = 4.1 kW (total 12 × 414 = 4.97 kW ✓). Ring heat capacity: 0.005 × 130 = 0.65 J/K, so the temperature rises about 856/0.65 ≈ 1300 K/s, and 100 K takes about 0.08 s. Key insight: a low-voltage source is not safe when the path resistance is tiny, and small objects have small heat capacity. Check: the real ring resistance and heat loss will differ, but the order of magnitude (tenths of a second to a painful burn) is right; hence "remove jewelry".</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>A camera-flash capacitor is 330 μF charged to 300 V. (a) How much energy is stored? (b) If the capacitor discharged through a body resistance of 1.0 kΩ (assume it stays constant), find the initial current, the time constant, and how long the current stays above the 10 mA let-go level.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) U = ½CV² = 0.5 × 330×10⁻⁶ × 300² = 14.9 J. (b) I₀ = V/R = 300/1000 = 0.30 A. τ = RC = 1000 × 330×10⁻⁶ = 0.33 s. The current falls as 0.30 e^(−t/τ); it reaches 10 mA when e^(−t/τ) = 1/30, so t = τ ln 30 = 0.33 × 3.40 = 1.12 s. Key insight: the stored energy is modest compared with the larger energies used in clinical defibrillation (on the order of 100 to 360 J), but it is delivered at a high voltage, and the current stays above the let-go level for more than a second in this idealized case. Real body resistance is not constant (it drops as tissue breaks down), so this is an order-of-magnitude estimate: treat flash capacitors as hazardous. Check: all 14.9 J is dissipated in the resistance eventually (∫I²R dt = ½CV²).</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>A 15 A appliance circuit has a wire terminal with 1.0 Ω of oxidized contact resistance (normal: 0.010 Ω). Find the heat generated at the joint for both cases, the voltage lost across the joint, and explain why the 15 A breaker does not trip.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Bad joint: P = I²R = 15² × 1.0 = 225 W, dropped voltage 15 V. Good joint: P = 225 × 0.010 = 2.25 W, 0.15 V. The heat rises by a factor 100 (P ∝ R at fixed current). The breaker does not trip because the current is set mainly by the load (here at most 15 A) and stays within the breaker's rating; the joint only adds heat locally. The breaker measures current, not hot spots. Key insight: overcurrent protection does not detect high-resistance faults; arc-fault circuit interrupters are designed for that. Check: 225 W concentrated on a few cubic centimetres of terminal quickly gets hot enough to char insulation.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>A bird perches on a bare overhead power line carrying 200 A. The 5.0 cm between its feet spans a length of conductor with resistance 1.0×10⁻⁴ Ω per metre. Estimate the voltage across the bird's feet and the current through the bird (body resistance 10 kΩ). Why is the bird safe, and what would make it unsafe?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>Voltage along the conductor: ΔV = I × (R per metre) × 0.05 m = 200 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.05 = 1.0×10⁻³ V = 1 mV. Current through the bird: 10⁻³/10⁴ = 10⁻⁷ A = 0.1 μA, a factor 10⁴ below perception (1 mA). The bird is safe because there is no <i>potential difference</i> of significance between its feet: both touch the same conductor. The line's tens of kV relative to ground is irrelevant until a path to ground or a second conductor exists. A wingspan reaching a second line (or a tower at ground potential) would supply the full line-to-line or line-to-ground voltage, with lethal consequences. Key insight: injury needs a path between two different potentials, and the conductor's tiny resistance makes the voltage along it negligible.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>A string of 50 identical series-wired decorative bulbs runs on 120 V; each bulb is rated 2.4 V. When a filament burns out, a shunt in the bulb base shorts it, so current continues. (a) What is the voltage and relative power (P ∝ V², ignoring changes in R) for the remaining bulbs after 1 bulb fails? (b) After 10 fail? (c) Explain why a string tends to fail in cascade.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) 49 bulbs share 120 V: 2.449 V each, 2.0% above rating; power ratio (2.449/2.4)² = 1.041 (+4.1%). (b) 40 bulbs: 3.0 V each, 25% above rating, power ratio 1.5625 (+56%). (c) Each failure raises the voltage on every survivor, which raises their power and filament temperature (and therefore their failure probability), which causes more failures, so the string deteriorates faster and faster. Key insight: in a series string the source voltage is fixed, so fewer elements means more voltage per element: a series analog of a positive feedback loop. Check: 50 × 2.4 = 120 V at start. Without shunts, one failure opens the circuit and the whole string goes dark (the lesson's "old Christmas lights").</div></details>

<h3>Trap list</h3>
<ul>
<li>"12 V cannot hurt": wrong if the path resistance is tiny (short circuit through metal) or the source can deliver hundreds of amps.</li>
<li>"It is only dangerous if the voltage is high." Current through the body, set by V/R, decides; the dry or wet state of the skin changes R by a factor 100.</li>
<li>"Unplugged means safe." Capacitors keep the charge, and it decays as e^(−t/RC); discharge through a resistor.</li>
<li>Sizing the fuse or breaker for the load rather than for the wire.</li>
<li>Thinking DC is always safer than AC. DC is not safe at high voltage, and it burns.</li>
<li>Connecting an ammeter across a source or using the wrong jack: a short circuit through the meter.</li>
<li>Assuming a bird on a line is hurt because the wire is at high voltage relative to ground; with no path to a second potential nothing flows.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Teach-back</h3>
<p>Explain to a friend in 3 sentences why a 12 V car battery can give a severe burn while a 120 V outlet may only tingle, using current and resistance (and I²R) rather than voltage alone.</p>
`;
DATA.chal['c10'] = `
<h3>Why it works</h3>
<p><b>RMS is defined by heating, which is why it is useful.</b> A resistor heats at P(t) = v(t)²/R. The average power is mean(v²)/R, and we define V_rms as the DC voltage giving the same heating: V_rms² = mean(v²), the <i>root</i> of the <i>mean</i> of the <i>square</i>. For v = V_p sin ωt, use sin²θ = (1 − cos 2θ)/2: the mean of sin² is ½, so V_rms = V_p/√2 = 0.707 V_p. The sine's instantaneous power swings from 0 to V_p²/R, which is twice its average, at <i>twice</i> the line frequency (a 60 Hz supply gives a bulb 120 flicker cycles per second). Units: rms volts have the same unit as DC volts, which is the point: P = V_rms²/R = I_rms²R = V_rms I_rms exactly as in DC.</p>
<p><b>A few waveforms worth knowing</b> (rms as a fraction of the peak value):</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Waveform</th><th>V_rms / V_p</th><th>Average of |v| / V_p</th></tr>
<tr><td>sine</td><td>0.707 (1/√2)</td><td>0.637 (2/π)</td></tr>
<tr><td>square (±V_p)</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>triangle</td><td>0.577 (1/√3)</td><td>0.5</td></tr>
<tr><td>full-wave rectified sine</td><td>0.707</td><td>0.637</td></tr>
<tr><td>half-wave rectified sine</td><td>0.5</td><td>0.318</td></tr>
<tr><td>pulse 0 to V_p, duty D</td><td>V_p √D</td><td>D</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Adding waveforms.</b> Mean of (v₁ + v₂)² = mean(v₁²) + mean(v₂²) + 2·mean(v₁v₂). The cross term is zero when the frequencies differ (or at the same frequency with 90° phase), so <i>rms values add in quadrature</i>: V_rms² = V₁² + V₂². A DC offset is one such component: V_rms² = V_DC² + V_AC,rms². In the same-frequency case, the cross term is ±V₁V₂ cos φ, giving a total between |V₁ − V₂| and V₁ + V₂.</p>
<p><b>The hot/neutral current balance (GFCI idea) is KCL.</b> Current out on the hot wire must equal current back on the neutral, unless some leaves through a third path, such as a person to ground. A ground-fault circuit interrupter compares the two currents; a difference of about 5 mA (far below anything a breaker could notice) opens the circuit within a fraction of a second. A breaker only trips on tens of amps.</p>
<p><b>Why 240 V for big loads.</b> Power P = VI. For the same P at twice the voltage the current halves, and the wire loss I²R_wire falls by a factor 4. That is also the reason the grid uses high-voltage transmission.</p>

<h3>Tools of thought</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Go through "same heating" for any waveform.</b> Square the waveform, average, take the root. For a pulse of duty D between 0 and V_p: V_rms = V_p√D.</li>
<li><b>Quadrature for different frequencies.</b> If two components have different frequencies, total power = sum of powers, so V_rms,total = √(V₁² + V₂²). DC offset is the same: √(V_DC² + V_ac,rms²).</li>
<li><b>Convert everything to rms or everything to peak</b> before comparing; meters read rms (for sines), scopes show peak or peak-to-peak.</li>
<li><b>Fraction-of-cycle estimation.</b> "When does v exceed a threshold?" Use sin θ₀ = V_th/V_p; the conducting angles are θ₀ to 180° − θ₀.</li>
<li><b>Current is what trips and what hurts.</b> Convert a power to current by I = P/V, then compare with the breaker rating and with hazard thresholds (about 10 mA let-go, 75 to 100 mA fibrillation in the lesson).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Challenge problems</h3>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>An oscilloscope shows a sine wave of amplitude 311 V (peak) at T = 20 ms applied to a 50 Ω resistor. Find (a) the frequency, (b) V_rms, (c) V_pp, (d) the average power, and (e) the peak instantaneous power.</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) f = 1/T = 50 Hz. (b) V_rms = 311/√2 = 220 V. (c) V_pp = 622 V. (d) P_avg = V_rms²/R = 220²/50 = 968 W (967 W with unrounded numbers). (e) The peak instantaneous power is V_p²/R = 311²/50 = 1934 W, exactly twice the average. Key insight: the average of sin² is ½, so the average power is half the peak power; RMS is the "effective DC" value. Check: P_avg = (V_p²/R)/2 = 967 W ✓. The meter on V~ reads 220 V, not 311 V.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Core</span>A 3000 W electric range (a) on a US 120 V circuit, (b) on a 240 V circuit. Find the current in each case and the ratio of the wire's I²R loss for the same wire. Would a 15 A breaker suffice?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) I = 3000/120 = 25 A. (b) I = 3000/240 = 12.5 A. Wire loss ratio: (25/12.5)² = 4, so the 120 V version wastes 4 times as much in the same wire. The 25 A case would trip a 15 A breaker; the 12.5 A case fits under 15 A (and in practice ranges have their own dedicated 240 V circuits with heavier wire). Key insight: doubling the voltage halves the current and quarters the wire loss at constant power. Check: 240 × 12.5 = 3000 W ✓.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>Each of the following voltage waveforms is applied in turn to a 50 Ω resistor. Find V_rms and the average power: (a) a pulse train between 0 and 10 V with 25% duty cycle; (b) a triangle wave between −10 V and +10 V; (c) a half-wave rectified sine of peak 10 V; (d) 5 V DC plus a sine of peak 5 V. Which heats the resistor most?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) V_rms = 10√0.25 = 5.00 V; P = 25/50 = 0.50 W. (The mean is 2.5 V, but the rms is bigger, because squaring emphasizes the high level.) (b) Triangle: V_rms = 10/√3 = 5.77 V; P = 33.3/50 = 0.667 W. (c) Half-wave: the mean of v² is V_p²/4 (the sine is zero half the time), so V_rms = 5.00 V; P = 0.50 W. (d) V_rms² = 5² + (5/√2)² = 25 + 12.5 = 37.5, V_rms = 6.12 V; P = 37.5/50 = 0.75 W, the most. Key insight: rms uses the mean of the square; DC and AC parts add in quadrature. All four values were confirmed numerically by sampling the waveforms. Check: a DC-only 5 V gives 0.50 W; adding the AC ripple raises it to 0.75 W (by 12.5/50 = 0.25 W).</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Hard</span>A GFCI outlet measures 12.000 A on the hot conductor and 11.990 A on the neutral. (a) How much current is leaking, and where might it be going? (b) If a person with wet skin (1 kΩ) touches the hot conductor while standing on grounded metal, what would the leak current be at 120 V, and would the 15 A breaker help? Would the GFCI (which trips at roughly 5 mA) trip in both cases?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) Leakage = 12.000 − 11.990 = 0.010 A = 10 mA, which returns by another path than the neutral: to ground through a person, a damaged appliance case, or wet insulation. By KCL, what goes out must come back. 10 mA is above the roughly 5 mA trip level, so the GFCI trips. (b) I = 120/1000 = 120 mA, which is in the fibrillation range. A 15 A breaker would not trip at 0.12 A (125 times below its rating). The GFCI sees 120 mA imbalance, far above 5 mA, and trips in a fraction of a second. Key insight: the breaker protects the wire from overcurrent; the GFCI protects people from a few-mA ground faults. They guard against different currents by factors of a thousand. Check: 15 A/0.12 A = 125; 120 mA/5 mA = 24 times the trip level.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>A neon indicator lamp is connected across 120 V rms, 60 Hz mains (through a current-limiting resistor). Assume the lamp conducts exactly when |v| exceeds 100 V (ignore hysteresis). For what fraction of each cycle is it conducting, and for how many milliseconds in each half cycle?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>V_p = 120√2 = 169.7 V. The lamp conducts when V_p|sin θ| > 100, so sin θ > 100/169.7 = 0.589, giving θ from 36.1° to 143.9° in each half cycle, a span of 107.8°. The fraction is 107.8/180 = 0.599 (about 60%). A half cycle lasts T/2 = 8.33 ms, so the lamp conducts for about 0.599 × 8.33 = 4.99 ms (about 5.0 ms) in each half cycle, and flashes twice per period (at 120 Hz on AC). Key insight: a threshold on a sine is an angle problem: θ₀ = arcsin(V_th/V_p). Check: a numerical sampling of one cycle gives 59.9%. Limiting cases: a threshold of 0 V gives 100%; a threshold of V_p gives 0%.</div></details>
<div class="prob"><span class="lv">Puzzle</span>Two sources are connected in series across a resistor. (a) A 120 V rms sine at 60 Hz and a 60 V rms sine at 60 Hz with a 90° phase shift: what is the total rms? (b) A 120 V rms sine at 60 Hz and another 120 V rms sine at 180 Hz, with any phase: total rms? (c) What range of total rms values is possible in (a) for any phase shift? (d) In a US split-phase service, what is the rms between the two hots and between either hot and neutral?</div><details class="sol"><summary></summary><div>(a) At 90° the cross term averages to zero: V_rms = √(120² + 60²) = 134 V. (b) Different frequencies: no cross term regardless of phase, so V_rms = √(120² + 120²) = 170 V (= 120√2). (c) Same-frequency sines: the extremes are in phase, 120 + 60 = 180 V, and opposite phase, 120 − 60 = 60 V. (d) The two hots are 180° apart, each 120 V rms relative to neutral, so hot-to-hot = 120 + 120 = 240 V rms, hot-to-neutral = 120 V. Key insight: the cross term 2V₁V₂cos φ decides everything; for unrelated frequencies the powers simply add. Check: v_hot1 − v_hot2 = V_p sin ωt − (−V_p sin ωt) = 2V_p sin ωt, so the rms doubles to 240 V.</div></details>

<h3>Trap list</h3>
<ul>
<li>Saying the US outlet is "120 V" but then using 120 V as the peak. The peak is 170 V; V_pp is 340 V.</li>
<li>Averaging a sine over a full cycle to get its "average voltage" (zero) instead of using rms for power.</li>
<li>Using V_rms = V_p/√2 for every waveform (square: 1, triangle: 1/√3, half-wave: 1/2).</li>
<li>Adding rms values of unrelated sources directly (120 + 120) instead of in quadrature (170).</li>
<li>Thinking a breaker protects people: it trips at amps, but shock injury starts at milliamps.</li>
<li>Putting a fuse or switch in the neutral: it must interrupt the hot leg, so the appliance is de-energized when it opens.</li>
<li>Believing a plug adapter changes voltage. It changes the shape of the plug only; a transformer/converter changes the voltage.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Teach-back</h3>
<p>Explain to a friend in 3 sentences why the 120 V on a wall outlet is an rms value, how it relates to the peak (about 170 V), and why rms values of independent waves add in quadrature.</p>
`;
})();
